\par\par \b \uc2 Biden Urges Broad Economic Plan --- President, in first speech to Congress, seeks $1.8 trillion for education, workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Catherine Lucey and Sabrina Siddiqui \par \uc2 1,229 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 April 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- President Biden declared "America is ready for a takeoff," as he pitched a sweeping vision for greater government investment to boost the economy, including a $1.8 trillion proposal for new spending on child care, education and paid leave.\par \par \uc2 Addressing a joint session of Congress for the first time as president, Mr. Biden sought to strike a hopeful tone just ahead of his 100th day in office, stressing his efforts to combat the pandemic, expand Covid-19 vaccinations -- which he urged people to get -- and spur economic growth.\par \par \uc2 "America is moving. Moving forward. And we can't stop now," he said Wednesday, in remarks that ran just over an hour. "We're in competition with China and other countries to win the 21st century."\par \par \uc2 The prime-time address looked different this year under pandemic restrictions, with a small group of masked attendees, no in-person guests hosted by the first lady and more muted applause. Mr. Biden used the moment to sell lawmakers and the public on his economic proposals, including his new American Families Plan, as well as renew his support for a long list of Democratic priorities, including passing legislation on policing, gun control and immigration.\par \par \uc2 "We have to prove democracy still works. That our government still works and we can deliver for our people," said Mr. Biden. He appealed to Republicans to work with him. GOP lawmakers have largely opposed his proposals, saying he has proposed too much government spending and that his tax plans could hurt the economy.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic president's economic proposals represent an ambitious effort to redefine the government's role in shaping the economy. Betting that government can be a driving force for growth, the White House is shifting away from long-held assumptions within both parties that the public sector is inherently less efficient than the private sector and that policy makers should generally defer to markets.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden highlighted the American Families Plan, which is paid for largely by raising taxes on the nation's wealthiest people, and his $2.3 trillion infrastructure package that includes new spending on bridges, roads and broadband internet. Mr. Biden cast the massive spending proposals as necessary to help the nation's economy and workers.\par \par \uc2 Referring to the family policies, Mr. Biden said it was time to make a "once-in-a-generation investment in our families and our children." He also said that the U.S. had used public spending on things like schools and space exploration to accomplish great things in the past.\par \par \uc2 "These are investments we made together as one country, and investments that only the government was in a position to make," Mr. Biden said. "Time and again, they propel us into the future."\par \par \uc2 To pay for all of his proposals, Mr. Biden would raise the top income-tax and capital-gains rates, increase taxes on companies and rely on an expanded \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  to audit and collect more money.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden stressed his campaign pledge that people making less than $400,000 wouldn't pay more taxes, but he said that it was time for companies and the wealthiest Americans to "pay their fair share."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden, who has attended numerous such addresses as both a U.S. senator and vice president, appeared at a Capitol still under heavy security following the deadly storming of the building on Jan. 6. Mr. Biden noted the recent violence, calling it "the worst attack on our democracy since the Civil War."\par \par \uc2 His speech marked the first time in the nation's history that both officeholders flanking the president on the dais were women: Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  (D., Calif.).\par \par \uc2 Sen. Tim Scott (R., S.C.) delivered the Republican rebuttal to the president's address. The only Black Republican in the Senate, Mr. Scott is considered a possible 2024 presidential candidate.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Scott called Mr. Biden's infrastructure proposal a "liberal wish list of government waste." He said the president has not lived up to his promises to bring the nation together.\par \par \uc2 In a direct appeal to Republicans, Mr. Biden argued that jobs and infrastructure were bipartisan issues and thanked a group of Republicans who have offered a counterproposal on infrastructure. "Let's get to work," he said.\par \par \uc2 He also urged Congress to "end our exhausting war over immigration," pushing for comprehensive changes to immigration laws amid a surge of migrants at the border.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden drew swift praise from Democrats, among them Mrs. Pelosi, who said he offered a "unifying message of resilience, resolve and hope."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden called for Congress to back many Democratic priorities, including raising the federal minimum wage to $15 an hour, closing the gender pay gap, and strengthening worker bargaining rights. He called for expanding access to healthcare and reducing prescription drug prices.\par \par \uc2 Following a series of mass shootings, Mr. Biden urged Congress to take action on gun control, and he asked lawmakers to pass a policing bill named for George Floyd, a Black man whose killing set off a wave of protests last year, by the anniversary of his May 25 death.\par \par \uc2 "We've all seen the knee of injustice on the neck of Black Americans," Mr. Biden said. "We have a giant opportunity to bend the arc of the moral universe toward justice."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden acknowledged tensions with Russia and China but said the U.S. should seek opportunities to cooperate on issues of mutual interest.\par \par \uc2 The president said his administration would pursue a coordinated strategy with U.S. allies to confront Iran and North Korea with "diplomacy and stern deterrence." And he defended his plan to withdraw U.S. forces from Afghanistan by Sept. 11. "After 20 years of value, valor and sacrifice, it's time to bring those troops home," Mr. Biden said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden's proposals will be tested in Congress, where Democrats hold thin majorities and the party's moderates and progressives aren't always aligned. If Republicans oppose his efforts in the Senate, Democrats would likely need to use a budget maneuver called reconciliation to advance the measure on a simple majority. That still requires Democrats to remain united in the chamber, which is split 50-50, with the vice president serving as a tiebreaker.\par \par \uc2 The White House said the new families proposal includes $1 trillion in new spending over 10 years and $800 billion in tax cuts, largely extensions of breaks created or expanded in this year's Covid-19 relief law.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden is calling for a universal preschool program for 3- and 4-year-olds and two years of tuition-free community college for all Americans.\par \par \uc2 He would also provide money to make child care more affordable for low- and middle-income families and establish a national paid-leave program for those needing time to care for a child or loved one or to recover from illness, among other reasons.\par \par \uc2 To pay for the new programs, the administration proposes raising the top income-tax rate to 39.6% from 37%.\par \par \uc2 For households making more than $1 million, Mr. Biden would also raise the top rate on capital-gains and dividends to 39.6% from 20%.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Richard Rubin and Rachel Louise Ensign contributed to this article.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw542\pich480\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8130\pichgoal7200
