\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: House Passes the Paycheck Fairness Act --- Bill seeking to narrow wage gap between men and women faces headwinds in Senate\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eric Morath and Andrew Duehren \par \uc2 592 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 April 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 House lawmakers approved legislation that would seek to narrow the gender-wage gap, a disparity that received increased attention a year after the coronavirus pandemic caused women to disproportionately lose their jobs.\par \par \uc2 The Paycheck Fairness Act would increase penalties for violations of a federal law that already prohibits wage discrimination on the basis of sex, enhance prohibitions to prevent retaliation against workers who lodge a discrimination complaint, and ban contracts that block employees from sharing their salary information.\par \par \uc2 The legislationpassed 217-210 over the objections of many Republicans, who argue that the law would lead to a flurry of spurious lawsuits from employees seeking higher pay. The bill faces headwinds in the evenly divided Senate, where Republicans have previously blocked similar legislation. One Republican joined Democrats Thursday to vote in favor of the bill.\par \par \uc2 Women's earnings as a share of men's pay has largely plateaued in the past decade, after years of narrowing. Last year, the median weekly earnings for a woman working full time was 82.4% that of a similar man, according to an analysis of Labor Department data. The gap is little changed since touching its narrowest point in 2014.\par \par \uc2 In addition to a persistent earnings disparity, women suffered more job lossthan men during the economic downturn caused by the coronavirus pandemic, losing 13.4 million jobs in March and April 2020, versus 12 million for men, according to the Labor Department. Women disproportionately held in-person service jobs at restaurants, hotels and certain retail stores that were shed when swaths of the economy were closed last spring.\par \par \uc2 "This pandemic has brought out the depth of our problems, exposed existing inequality and threatened women's economic security at a disproportionate rate," said Rep. Rosa DeLauro (D., Conn.), a lead sponsor of the bill.\par \par \uc2 Many Republicans contend that the pay gap is the result of different employment choices women make, not discrimination in the workplace. \uc2 Rep. Virginia Foxx\uc2  (R., N.C.) said the legislation would only empower lawyers seeking to sue employers for the wage practices. "The Paycheck Fairness Act is not a win for women in the slightest," she said.\par \par \uc2 Researchers are split on the degree to which the gender-wage gap is due to employer discrimination versus other factors.\par \par \uc2 Gender-based pay discrimination has been illegal since 1963 but is still a widespread practice, said \uc2 Jocelyn Frye\uc2 , a senior fellow at the left-leaning \uc2 Center for American Progress\uc2  think tank, where she studies women's issues. The bill, she said, would limit employers' ability to cite market conditions or salary history as justification for pay disparities. "This bill would tighten existing loop holes and help remove discrimination as a factor for pay differences," she said.\par \par \uc2 Other factors also influence the pay gap. Women have historically been more likely to take jobs such as home health aides and child-care workers, which tend to offer lower pay than predominantly male jobs, such as those in construction and manufacturing. Women who work full-time work fewer hours a week, according to the Labor Department.\par \par \uc2 Mark Perry\uc2 , an economist at the right-leading \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 , said the earnings difference reflects women choosing more family-friendly employment over maximizing wages. "Men and women make different decisions as far as their families, careers and college majors," he said. "As long as they continue to do so, the earnings disparity will remain."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210416eh4g0001c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
