\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: New Team Nominated For Border Policy Jobs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Hackman \par \uc2 528 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 April 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The White House will nominate two critics of the Trump administration's border policies to lead key immigration agencies inside the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 , as a surge of migrants at the southern border has become an early challenge for the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 Chris Magnus, the police chief in Tucson, Ariz., will be nominated to lead \uc2 U.S. Customs and Border Protection\uc2 , the agency that oversees the Border Patrol and immigration and customs checks at airports.\par \par \uc2 Ur Jaddou, a longtime Democratic immigration-policy official, will be nominated to head \uc2 U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services\uc2 , the primary agency in charge of legal immigration as well as refugee and asylum processing.\par \par \uc2 The two are among a slate of six officials named by the White House on Monday to be nominated for posts in the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 . They include the administration's choice to be deputy DHS secretary, John Tien, who worked at the National Security Council in the Obama White House, where he specialized in policy on Afghanistan and Pakistan.\par \par \uc2 The nominees also include two senior cybersecurity officials. Jen Easterly, a former senior counterterrorism and cybersecurity official at the \uc2 National Security Agency\uc2 , is being tapped to lead DHS's cybersecurity branch. Rob Silvers, who served at DHS under the Obama administration, including as a senior aide to then-Deputy Secretary \uc2 Alejandro Mayorkas\uc2 , will be nominated to lead the department's policy office.\par \par \uc2 The White House will also nominate Jon Meyer, another former Obama administration DHS official, to return as the department's general counsel.\par \par \uc2 The nominations begin to fill out an agency that, under the Trump administration, had been marked by vacant posts filled by acting officials, often for multiple years.\par \par \uc2 President Biden had pledged during the campaign to quickly install Senate-confirmed leaders across the agency's immigration posts, but it has taken his administration nearly three months to tap the nominees.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2 , the third DHS immigration agency that is charged with arresting and deporting immigrants, remained without a permanent leader through the four years of the Trump administration. A nominee to lead that agency wasn't included among the six named Monday, reflecting the Biden administration's difficulty in finding a suitable candidate who would please Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Magnus and Ms. Jaddou both emerged under the previous administration as prominent critics of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's immigration policies.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Magnus, who gained a reputation as a progressive police chief, criticized policies that he felt damaged trust between the police and communities they guarded, with Latino residents afraid to report crimes or seek help.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Magnus joined a bipartisan group of law-enforcement officials to support what they called the Biden administration's more humane approach to immigration enforcement.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Jaddou, who had worked as the top lawyer at the legal-immigration agency under the Obama administration, later formed a watchdog group called DHS Watch, a platform she used to critique the Trump administration's many changes to legal immigration, including how work visas and asylum applications were being handled.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Rachael Levy contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210413eh4d00011\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
