\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Alabama Vote Upholds Long-Term Trend\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eric Morath and Lauren Weber \par \uc2 697 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 April 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers' rejection of a union at \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 .'s warehouse in Bessemer, Ala., is a setback to organized labor's efforts to reverse a decadeslong decline in private-sector membership nationally.\par \par \uc2 The Alabama result underscores unions' challenges in increasing membership in the U.S. private sector, where they represent just 6.3% of workers, down from 24.2% in 1973, according to data from \uc2 Georgia State University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hiring at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  -- the second-largest private employer in the U.S. -- and other e-commerce warehouses increased last year even as the country shed millions of jobs, including more than 300,000 union positions, during the pandemic. For unions, the time appeared ripe to organize workers in an expanding sector and an environment where labor unions traditionally have operated: a large blue-collar site where many employees do similar jobs.\par \par \uc2 The effort failed despite President Biden's endorsement, his stated goal of creating more union jobs and a renewed embrace of labor by many congressional Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Last year, more union members worked for the government than for private-sector employers, according to the Labor Department, showing the public sector is now the stronghold of organized labor. Teacher strikes and protests in 2018 and 2019 won pay increases and other concessions in Arizona, West Virginia, Los Angeles and other states and cities, and more recently, educator unions influenced plans to reopen schools during the pandemic in Chicago and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 The Amazon vote bodes poorly for organized labor trying to increase the share of workers who are union members and revive organized labor as a formidable voice in American workplaces, said Jonathan Spitz, co-leader of the labor relations practice at Jackson Lewis, a management-side law firm. Union members accounted for 10.8% of the total U.S. workforce last year, down from 24% in 1973, according to Georgia State's data.\par \par \uc2 Organizing workers at major employers such as \uc2 Amazon\uc2  or \uc2 Walmart Inc\uc2 . has long been a "holy grail" for unions, he said, but many big companies have the power and the capital to pay competitively, survey employees about their experiences and respond to dissatisfaction before it grows.\par \par \uc2 "Employers control the economic issues, they decide what pay and benefits look like and if they want to pre-empt organizing based on economic issues, they can do it," he said.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  told its workers in Alabama that unionizing wasn't necessary, highlighting that it starts workers at $15 an hour -- more than double the state's minimum wage of $7.25 an hour, the federal minimum -- and the healthcare benefits it offers employees. The median pay in the U.S. for warehouse and storage workers was $17.77 an hour last year, according to the Labor Department.\par \par \uc2 The organizing union -- the Retail, Wholesale and Department Store Union -- sought to bargain over work rules and better pay, frequently citing the wealth of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  founder \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Workers at the Bessemer warehouse overwhelmingly rejected unionization, with 71% casting ballots not to join the union, according to the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The failure at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  comes after other unsuccessful attempts to organize large companies. United Food and Commercial Workers had sought to unionize \uc2 Walmart\uc2  stores and labor unions backed the Fight for $15 protests against \uc2 McDonald's Corp\uc2 . and other chains. While those protests influenced state and local minimum-wage changes, it failed to secure new union members.\par \par \uc2 "One of the concerns here for people who'd like to see something change is they spent a lot of money and energy on it and it didn't work, so there's some reluctance to try it again with other retail giants," said Ruth Milkman, a sociologist and chair of the labor studies department at the CUNY School of Labor and Urban Studies.\par \par \uc2 The organizing difficulties faced by private-sector unions stand in contrast to those in the public sector. More than 40% of local-government workers, including teachers, firefighters and police, are union members. In some cases, those unions receive widespread public support and are often more aligned with their management.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Amara Omeokwe contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210412eh4c00016\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
