\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Nurses' Pay Sparks Litigation --- Hospital chain, agency supplying healthcare workers file claims related to pandemic\b0\par\par\uc2 By Melanie Evans and Jim Carlton \par \uc2 778 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Hospital chain Steward Health Care Systems LLC and staffing firm Aya Healthcare Inc. are suing each other over outstanding bills in a dispute over soaring wages for nurses in the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Aya, based in San Diego, claims the Dallas-based hospital chain owes it more than $40 million including overdue bills, according to a lawsuit Aya filed Tuesday in Massachusetts Superior Court. Steward last month sued Aya in the same court, alleging price gouging and seeking to stop the staffing firm from canceling work for its nurses scheduled at the hospital chain.\par \par \uc2 Steward alleged in its suit in early March that rates charged by Aya were "illegally inflated" and asked a judge to temporarily stop the staffing firm from calling off work for nurses employed at or soon to arrive at Steward hospitals.\par \par \uc2 One Steward hospital in Houston stopped taking ambulances and had to reschedule heart procedures without the temporary staff it had scheduled, according to Steward's court filings.\par \par \uc2 A judge agreed but also ordered Steward to post a bond of $10 million to offset the risk to Aya.\par \par \uc2 Aya, which contracts with other agencies to fill some jobs, notified the agencies it could no longer pay bills each month because of Steward's growing balance, said Aya Chief Executive Officer Alan Braynin. "We do not know what supplier companies did or said to their employees, or if staff were actually pulled," he said. "We do know that Aya did not pull nurses midshift or midassignment," Mr. Braynin said.\par \par \uc2 Steward denies it owes Aya $40 million. Steward General Counsel Herbert Holtz said in an interview that Steward has paid the staffing agency more than $39 million under their agreement.\par \par \uc2 He said that includes an estimated $11 million the hospital chain believes it is owed because Aya overcharged for services. Aya rejected Mr. Holtz's claims.\par \par \uc2 The court's order directing Aya to send staff to Steward ended Sunday.\par \par \uc2 This week, Aya said it began reassigning nurses to assignments at other hospitals or healthcare facilities.\par \par \uc2 Hourly rates for Aya staff soared to $160 from rates before the pandemic for temporary medical workers, such as nurses, that were typically around $75, Steward alleged in its suit. Aya's rising prices violated a Massachusetts law that, starting last August, capped travel-nurse rates, according to the hospital chain's suit.\par \par \uc2 Aya denies it violated Massachusetts law. In its complaint, the staffing agency alleged 90% of bills that Steward disputes were for services outside the state.\par \par \uc2 The Massachusetts Attorney General's office said it is monitoring the litigation between Steward and Aya.\par \par \uc2 Aya in its lawsuit defended its rates and said they are competitive, in line with markets and amounts Steward agreed to pay. "The supply of travel nurses is limited," the suit said. "There are only so many nurses."\par \par \uc2 The staffing agency said in its suit it repeatedly asked Steward to pay a growing balance. It sought a payment plan, according to the suit. In early March, Steward proposed payments of $1 million to $1.5 million a week, but the staffing firm's costs at the hospital chain averaged $2.5 million a week, Aya claimed.\par \par \uc2 Steward has tangled with lawmakers and in court over its operations in recent years, including in March 2020, when the company threatened to close its struggling Easton Hospital in Pennsylvania unless it received $40 million from the state. It got $8 million and months later sold the hospital to St. Luke's University Health Network. Steward said the hospital's expenses and liabilities for the year totaled $28 million after it received state aid and before it was sold.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Travel-Nurse Rates\par \par \uc2 Soared in Pandemic\par \par \uc2 Healthcare labor was one of many markets to see prices jump last year, as the coronavirus pandemic created global supply disruptions or shortages for everything ranging from crops to ingredients in hand sanitizer to toilet paper to protective masks worn by hospital staff.\par \par \uc2 Across the travel-nurse industry -- an estimated $6 billion market in 2019, according to Staffing Industry Analysts -- rates shot up as Covid-19 surged, according to researchers and hospital and government officials who scrambled to find more staff.\par \par \uc2 Travel-nurse wages nationally rose 25% in April as one of the earliest surges overran New York City hospitals, researchers from the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  and \uc2 Yale University\uc2  found in an analysis of data from Health Carousel LLC, one of the largest U.S. health staffing companies.\par \par \uc2 Their study found rising wages attracted staff. Nurses entered the market and traveled farther for higher pay.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210409eh490001m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
