\par\par \b \uc2 MANSION --- Taking a Chance on Vegas --- Young families and coastal residents looking for a new way of life are flocking to Sin City\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nancy Keates \par \uc2 1,533 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par M1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When Ike Pyun bought his new house in Las Vegas this past December, it checked all the boxes on his wish list: It was in a secure, gated community with lots of young families, had a number of parks nearby where his son could grow up playing, and there was more space so he and his wife, Kelsey Pyun, could both work from home. It was also near the Strip, with easy parking and great options for date nights.\par \par \uc2 The kicker: The monthly mortgage payment was half what he was paying in rent in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 "The contrast was shocking," says the 41-year-old investor and entrepreneur. He and his wife, a real-estate agent, paid $615,000 for a four-bedroom, four-bathroom, 3,400-square-foot house at the edge of Red Rock Canyon National Conservation Area.\par \par \uc2 Las Vegas is having another moment. Built up by gangsters, known for its seedy side, this city of some 650,000 people is spread over 135 square miles, with an assortment of small, older neighborhoods close to downtown and large, master-planned, gated communities with parks and shops on the outskirts centered around golf courses and artificial lakes. Located in the middle of the Nevada desert, the city was a face of the 2008 housing crisis, with whole neighborhoods of empty, foreclosed homes. Now, Sin City clocks the highest share of page views on Zillow from out-of-town buyers of any major city. Median home prices were up 12.3% in February compared with a year earlier, according to Las Vegas Realtors. The city was the most overvalued large metro area in the country in the fourth quarter of 2020, according to a ranking by credit-rating agency \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Amid all the troubles the pandemic has wrought on the local economy, luxury real estate is a bright spot for Las Vegas. The number of homes over $1 million that sold in 2020 was 45% higher than in 2019. On top of that, the revitalization of its downtown, which started with the Arts District, has continued with a new resort and casino opening in October, the first built downtown since 1980. Developers are eyeing roughly 100 properties, including vacant lots, motels, commercial buildings and apartments, recently put on the market by the estate of the late Zappos founder Tony Hsieh. Mr. Hsieh had been a major player in Las Vegas real estate and was a major developer, investor and booster of the city.\par \par \uc2 Las Vegas real-estate agents say many of their clients are now two-income families and a high percentage are coming from California. While the city has long been popular with people from Southern California, what's new is a significant increase in interest from people living in the Bay Area, says Michael McGraw of Elite Realty. Mr. McGraw says they tend to want guarded, gated communities, along with the zero state income tax in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 "The younger crowd is finding its way here," says Harvey Tadmor, a broker with Real Simple Real Estate. The city's Economic and Urban Development Department started running digital marketing ads in November in the Bay Area and Seattle, tweeting "Tell your friends: If they are remote working, they should move here: 300 + days of sunshine, pro sports, no income taxes, world class hospitality and dining." ("And hookers," one person tweeted in response.)\par \par \uc2 Cory Rose, 37, and her husband Austin Rose, 26, both of whom work in tech, moved from the Bay Area to Vegas in November with their 16-month-old son. They bought a 2,000-square-foot three-bedroom, two-bathroom home on a quarter acre for $470,000 in a downtown neighborhood in Vegas called McNeil Estates, a historic district composed of about 500 modest ranch and Midcentury Modern houses built in the 1950s and 1960s. The homes have tidy patches of green lawns and palm trees. The intimate neighborhood is about five minutes from the iconic Strip, with its massive, global-themed resorts and casinos, and five minutes from the Arts District downtown, where buildings are painted with bright murals and large modern sculptures sit near vacant lots of old motels. It is also close to the city's Smith Center for the Performing Arts, the Discovery Children's Museum and the Downtown Container Park, a open-air center with small shops and restaurants in metal cubes and shipping containers, fronted by a 40-foot tall metal sculpture of a praying mantis that shoots liquid propane-fueled flames six stories high.\par \par \uc2 The Roses' mortgage is $2,300 a month, half what they were paying in monthly rent in Emeryville, Calif., (between Oakland and Berkeley) for a 1,200-square-foot, two-bedroom, two-bathroom apartment.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Rose never considered herself an outdoorsy person -- she owns a T-shirt that says "I'm Indoorsy." Now that they have moved to Vegas, they go hiking every weekend in the mountains and canyons just west of the city. They even own a bike rack for their car. She and her husband created a list with what they wanted in a city: A professional sports team, good schools, a reasonable cost of living, fun things to do, a Pacific Time zone, and a house within 30 minutes of a downtown. The other options, which included Boise, Idaho, Portland, Ore. and Denver, didn't check all the boxes like Vegas, she says. Ms. Rose has moved a number of times, from Washington, D.C., to Atlanta, but, she says "this is permanent."\par \par \uc2 "It's nothing like people think it is," says Farai Kasambira, 43, who moved to Summerlin, a master-planned community within the city limits of Las Vegas next to the Spring Mountains and Red Rock Canyon, in May from Irvine, Calif. The co-founder of a tech company, he was paying $2,500 a month for a one-bedroom apartment. In Vegas, he pays the same amount to rent a three-bedroom house with a pool in the massive gated community, which sprawls across 22,500 acres, has over 200 parks and includes a mixture of condos, townhomes and large single-family houses. There are fitness centers, restaurants, shops, several golf courses and over 20 schools, both private and public. What he likes best is being able to live in the suburbs but with easy access to the Arts District downtown, which, he says, is diverse and full of energy.\par \par \uc2 It was a vibe of resiliency and hospitality that drew Teddy Liaw, 42, CEO of a call center technology company, to Vegas from San Francisco. He bought a six-bedroom, nine-bathroom, 6,952-square-foot house for $2.2 million in January. Sitting on half an acre near a golf course, the house has a three-car garage, a wine cellar, a 400-gallon saltwater aquarium, two pools and a pool house.\par \par \uc2 "You feel immediately that friendliness is in the culture," he says. "I feel motivated to make a difference here." In San Francisco, he felt bogged down by what he says was rampant crime and open drug use on the streets. In Vegas, "it feels safe. There's peace of mind."\par \par \uc2 Vegas is rapidly facing the same drawbacks as the cities people are leaving. In 2020, it was the second-fastest growing city in net population gains, relative to its population size, for the year by Updater, a relocation app. The homicide rate is up 100% in 2021, with 26 murders as of March 26, up from 13 during the same period a year earlier, according to Metro police.\par \par \uc2 Real-estate agents also expect home prices to keep rising, in part because supply is so tight. By the end of February, LVR reported a 60% year-over-year drop in the number of single-family homes listed for sale without an offer, according to LVR. "It's an extreme seller's market," says LVR president Aldo Martinez. One plus for living in Las Vegas is relatively low property tax rates. For the Las Vegas metro area, LVR estimates the rate is at 0.9% of value. Nevada has a tax cap, which mandates that property taxes can go up only a certain amount each year, and the sale of a home doesn't trigger the reassessment of a property.\par \par \uc2 Mackenzie Johnson feels like he got in just in time. In September 2019, Mr. Johnson, 30, who is in the music business, bought a 3,400-square-foot, four-bedroom house with a swimming pool in Summerlin near a golf course for $990,000. It was "mind-blowingly cheaper" than the equivalent in San Diego, where he was living.\par \par \uc2 It was also less expensive than Austin, Texas, where many of his friends relocated, he says. Austin might be hipper, but he likes the family feel of Vegas where he hangs out with his golfing buddies and is looking forward to starting a family.\par \par \uc2 "I thought I would stay a few years," he says. "But it's been the best move of my life."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210409eh4900002\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
