\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Careers & Leadership: Hunting for a Job? How to Write A Cover Letter That Stands Out\b0\par\par\uc2 By Allison Pohle \par \uc2 815 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 April 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Looking for job postings, updating the resume and sending follow-up emails are all draining tasks, but, for some, writing a cover letter is the most overwhelming part of a job application.\par \par \uc2 I've edited many cover letters for family members, friends and acquaintances. They often ask whether spending time writing a cover letter is worth the effort. In a survey by ResumeGo, a company that offers resume-writing services, 87% of hiring professionals said they do read cover letters.\par \par \uc2 Here are tips from experts and my own playbook for writing a compelling cover letter.\par \par \uc2 Put some thought into it\par \par \uc2 Job seekers often rush when writing cover letters, says Dorie Clark, a professor at Duke University's Fuqua School of Business. But they are worth spending time on. "They're actually critical in terms of shaping your narrative because it's your opportunity to tell the world who you are, what you're about, and why you're qualified," she says.\par \par \uc2 But it's OK to use a formula\par \par \uc2 The structure can be simple. A typical cover letter includes the greeting, an introduction, a couple of body paragraphs, a closing paragraph and your signature.\par \par \uc2 Your first paragraph should explain who you are and why you're interested in the job. Use the middle section to prove that you are qualified. In the final part, bring home why you're a good fit for the position.\par \par \uc2 Grab attention with the first paragraph\par \par \uc2 Employers want to see something that will stand out from commonly used introductions like, "I am writing to you to apply for the office-manager position." Instead, you could explain how a specific professional or personal experience inspired you to advance your career. Keep the anecdote to two sentences. You can complete the paragraph by tying that story to the job: "It's because of this experience that I am excited to be applying for [insert job title here] at [insert company name]."\par \par \uc2 Differentiate the letter from your resume\par \par \uc2 It can be tempting to copy and paste from your resume to describe your work history, but your cover letter is an opportunity to present your experience in relation to the job you want. Use one or two paragraphs in the body of the cover letter to demonstrate how your accomplishments have prepared you to meet the requirements of the new role.\par \par \uc2 Play up your skills\par \par \uc2 Identify the skills you have and how they would be useful for the position. If you're applying for an entry-level position, or don't have a lot of experience, emphasize your strengths, Ms. Clark advises.\par \par \uc2 Maybe you're a self-starter who can meet goals even while working independently in this era of widespread remote work. Or maybe you come from a nontraditional professional background, which you can frame as an asset because of the skills or perspective it has given you. "If you're able to make the case, 'Look, I come from this different place, which is unusual in your field, and that's exactly what you need,' that can be really quite powerful," Ms. Clark says.\par \par \uc2 Emphasize what you can do for the company\par \par \uc2 Use the latter part of your letter to tie together how your skills, experience and background make you an ideal candidate for the job. For example, after outlining some of your relevant professional history you could add: "These experiences have prepared me to jump right into the data-driven decision-making position at [insert company here], which would make me a valuable asset to the team."\par \par \uc2 Consider other formats\par \par \uc2 It can be appropriate to use bullet points in certain circumstances, such as listing your skills, says Dana Leavy-Detrick, founder and director of Brooklyn Resume Studio. After one or two sentences outlining your interest in the role or organization, you could say: "At this point in my career, I'm confident I can offer" followed by bullet points describing your abilities.\par \par \uc2 End with why you want to work for the company\par \par \uc2 The final paragraph is your last chance to say why you want the job. It is important to strike a tone that shows you are interested, but not too pushy. You can write a sentence or two that emphasizes your interest in the role or company, and say you hope to hear back soon. Avoid writing that you will follow up over the phone or reach out to schedule a time to talk.\par \par \uc2 End with a sign-off that strikes the appropriate tone, such as "Sincerely," as opposed to "Thanks," which may be too informal.\par \par \uc2 Proofread before sending\par \par \uc2 Employers prefer cover letters that are less than a page long, so make trims if necessary. Read it aloud and check it carefully for any errors. Then you can hit submit.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210405eh450000q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
