\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Careers & Leadership -- At Work: Offices Are Back -- in Australia --- The country, where cases are low, offers a glimpse of what post-pandemic offices might be like\b0\par\par\uc2 By Krithika Varagur \par \uc2 1,007 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 April 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When Shant Soghomonian, a senior sales director at Dell, went back to his office in Sydney last month after a year of remote work, the space looked the same as he remembered, and he didn't even have to wear a mask. But when he stepped into a sanitized conference room for his first in-person meeting of 2021, he realized he had forgotten how to greet colleagues in person.\par \par \uc2 "Do you shake their hands? Do you do the elbow bump?" recalls Mr. Soghomonian. A consensus eventually arose around "predominantly elbows," he says.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Soghomonian and about 2,000 of his \uc2 Dell\uc2  colleagues in Australia have been able to go in-person to their offices for months. On March 9, \uc2 Dell\uc2 's six Australia offices entered their third and final phase of reopening. Meanwhile, about 41,000 \uc2 Dell\uc2  employees in the U.S. remain fully remote -- at "phase 0" -- for the near future, according to a Dell spokesperson.\par \par \uc2 Australians' return to work gives a taste of what the post-pandemic office might look like in other countries soon. The country of nearly 26 million people has been at the vanguard of the world-wide effort to reopen offices. Its strict early lockdown, intensive contact-tracing and aggressive quarantine protocols have helped keep daily new cases very low for months and total number of deaths under 1,000 since the pandemic started, according to its Department of Health. Offices of multinational firms like \uc2 Adobe\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , and \uc2 Dell\uc2  started to reopen as early as June.\par \par \uc2 But it hasn't been a straight line from quarantine to unmasked meetings. Most offices had to slowly phase back re-entry, prepare for workers' skittishness in communal spaces and in meetings, and counter people's reluctance to commute again.\par \par \uc2 Earlier accommodations to a highly contagious virus still shape office life. \uc2 Dell\uc2  still uses a desk reservation system and there are no more cookie jars in the break room, says Angela Fox, a Sydney-based managing director. Hand-sanitizer pumps are everywhere, meeting room doors are tagged with signs to indicate that they've been sanitized, and the offices' air-handling units have upped their fresh air intake from 15% to 40% for better ventilation. \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , which has two offices in Australia, cut its buffet and now serves workers plated meals, says \uc2 Will Easton\uc2 , the company's managing director for Australia and New Zealand.\par \par \uc2 "Back in November, people were afraid to touch shared milk cartons for their coffee or be in a lift together, which caused logistical nightmares in a skyscraper," recalls Robbie Robertson, a Sydney-based managing partner of consulting giant Deloitte, which has over 9,000 employees in Australia and started reopening in October to general staff.\par \par \uc2 Given Australia's extremely cautious approach to new Covid cases, even a tiny number of new infections can lead states into short, sudden lockdowns. Just last week, \uc2 Dell\uc2  temporarily closed its Brisbane office when the city declared a three-day lockdown to curb several new Covid cases, says Ms. Fox.\par \par \uc2 Alongside Australia, offices in other places that have controlled the pandemic well, like New Zealand and Taiwan, have also been open for months. They share several factors, like robust healthcare systems and continuously low infection rates, that help them pass the risk assessment tools used by multinational firms like \uc2 Dell\uc2  and \uc2 Adobe\uc2  to help decide whether to reopen a site.\par \par \uc2 At most large firms, in-person work remains voluntary, rather than mandated.\par \par \uc2 "It's still important to keep the hybrid option available, because that's what workers say they want," says Mr. Robertson at Deloitte.\par \par \uc2 But working from home may not be possible forever. A BCG report found that 63% of 1,002 Australian employees across a range of companies wanted to continue some form of remote work as of June 2020. But only 40% of 121 employers surveyed last December believed they would work in hybrid models in the future. \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , whose Sydney and Melbourne offices are currently at 50% capacity, is "looking to remove the opt-in process" for returning to the office in Australia and New Zealand as they move toward 100% reopening, sometime after July 2021, says Mr. Easton. (There are also plenty of Australians in industries like healthcare and retail who haven't had a choice to work remotely.)\par \par \uc2 For multinational firms that have reopened for voluntary in-person work, phased return is a common theme. Nearly all of \uc2 Dell\uc2 's offices in 73 countries were at phase 0 last spring, meaning everyone was remote. Phase 1, which \uc2 Dell\uc2  initiated in Australia last June, allowed for about 10% of the workforce to return. Phase 2, which started between August and December, allowed workers back at 50% capacity, and phase 3 allowed anyone back, without masks or social distancing requirements. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  has six such phases and is in phase 5 in Australia, which allows workers back with some stipulations like completing a health declaration form and wearing masks while moving around, says Ingrid Jenkins, HR head of \uc2 Microsoft Australia\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It can take some creativity to convince colleagues who became comfortable in their home offices that in-person work is worth the trek. Mr. Robertson, of Deloitte, has been experimenting this year with incentives for his team of about 230 people.\par \par \uc2 He put snacks like fruit and candy back on the floor, created "Team Tuesday" meetings to share achievements, and instituted "Thirsty Thursday" happy hours.\par \par \uc2 "I want people to have FOMO [the fear of missing out] for not coming in," he says. It seems to be working: He says that since January, the portion of his team using the office jumped from about 20% to over 60%.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks ago, he says, over 30 people stayed for drinks at a nearby pub until 6:30 p.m. "Everyone was like, this is so great -- it's almost like being in 2019."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210405eh450000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
