\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- The New Executive Suite --- MetLife's CEO is working in a converted closet at home; Everest Re's boss commutes to nearly empty company offices. 15 leaders reflect on a pandemic work year.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emily Glazer and Francesca Fontana \par \uc2 1,034 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Hilton Worldwide Holdings Inc. Chief Executive \uc2 Chris Nassetta\uc2  worked from home in Arlington, Va., with his wife, six daughters and two dogs for two weeks before returning to the hotel chain's nearly empty headquarters for the rest of the past year. Sharmistha Dubey has been leading \uc2 Match Group Inc\uc2 . from her dining room table near Dallas. \uc2 Herman Miller\uc2 's \uc2 Andi Owen\uc2  has her dog Finn to keep her company while working from her home office in Grand Rapids, Mich.\par \par \uc2 Moderna Inc\uc2 . CEO \uc2 Stephane Bancel\uc2  relishes twice-daily 30-minute walks between his home in Boston and the vaccine maker's Cambridge offices, where he resumed working in August, so he can crystallize his priorities and reflect on the day.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal photographed them and 11 other business leaders in their pandemic office spaces. More than a year after the coronavirus upended the way we work, the heads of companies in sectors including finance, hospitality and technology spoke about what they've learned from the largely remote year, what challenges they faced and what changes they plan to leave in place during the next phase of work.\par \par \uc2 Many said they have found that more communication, flexibility and transparency have been crucial in staying connected to their employees.\par \par \uc2 These are edited excerpts from interviews conducted with the business leaders in their current work spaces.\par \par \uc2 Chris Hyams, job-search site Indeed\par \par \uc2 Working from his home office in Texas\par \par \uc2 "I was flying 200,000 miles a year for the last six or seven years. And sitting in this one room on Zoom, I am more connected with everyone in the business than I've ever been -- because everyone is in the same place. We're all just squares on a screen."\par \par \uc2 Nandita Bakhshi\uc2 , \uc2 Bank of the West\par \par \uc2 Working from her home office in New Jersey\par \par \uc2 "We got 600 volunteers signed up overnight that left their day job, learned how to do a [Paycheck Protection Program]. . . . That happened within hours."\par \par \uc2 Juan Andrade\uc2 , insurer Everest Re Group Ltd.\par \par \uc2 Working from Everest's New Jersey office\par \par \uc2 "We're built for responding to a typhoon or to an earthquake or to a hurricane. . . . You look inward and then you apply a lot of those lessons to how you run the company."\par \par \uc2 Brad Karp, law firm \uc2 Paul, Weiss, Rifkind, Wharton & Garrison LLP\par \par \uc2 Working from his home office in New York\par \par \uc2 "Waking up every morning and going to sleep every night in your office quickly becomes old. Creating reasonable boundaries and worrying constantly about my colleagues' mental health and stress have become critical priorities."\par \par \uc2 Andi Owen\uc2 , furniture company \uc2 Herman Miller Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Working from her home office in Michigan\par \par \uc2 "I think productivity in the future is going to be much more a measure of results, rather than activities."\par \par \uc2 Julia Hartz\uc2 , event-ticketing company \uc2 Eventbrite Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Working from her home office in California\par \par \uc2 "We're starting to talk more about how access to technology or balancing your home and work lives in this reality has been very challenging."\par \par \uc2 Sharmistha Dubey, online-dating giant \uc2 Match Group Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Working from her dining room in Texas\par \par \uc2 "In the Zoom world, you can get a lot of things done, but you have to ask for it. There are very few serendipitous moments. It's almost as if there is a scripted narrative."\par \par \uc2 Michel A. Khalaf\uc2 , life-insurance company \uc2 Metlife Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Working from his converted-closet office in New York\par \par \uc2 "In a way, we had to move much faster than we normally work because that was the only way for us to deliver for our customers during the pandemic."\par \par \uc2 David McCormick\uc2 , hedge fund \uc2 Bridgewater Associates LP\par \par \uc2 Working from his kitchen in Colorado\par \par \uc2 "I think we're all going to go back to work . . . with a sense that work is a real privilege. It's a real privilege to be able to go to the office and be with your colleagues."\par \par \uc2 Chris Nassetta\uc2 , hotel chain \uc2 Hilton Worldwide Holdings Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Working from Hilton's Virginia office\par \par \uc2 "I wasn't really built for this. I really like being with our people and it gives me a huge amount of energy. And I hope that when I'm with them, that I give them some energy. . . . But when I'm sitting here doing Zoom calls all day, it's hard to really tap into that."\par \par \uc2 Lynn Good\uc2 , electricity and gas company \uc2 Duke Energy Corp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Working from her home office in North Carolina\par \par \uc2 "We have remarked over and over about what an extraordinary time it has been. But it truly has. I mean, it has threatened health; it has created loss; people have had issues with how to manage their work, their families, their schooling -- just everything. And at the same time social unrest [and a] tough political season all coming together."\par \par \uc2 Adena Friedman\uc2 , exchange operator Nasdaq Inc.\par \par \uc2 Working from \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2 's New York office\par \par \uc2 "We're in a temporary situation so we try very hard to avoid making decisions that we'll later realize were not the right ones for the organization."\par \par \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2 , restaurant chain \uc2 Chipotle Mexican Grill Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Working from Chipotle's California office\par \par \uc2 "There's just value in every four or five weeks getting everybody on the phone together. . . . It's really important for our kitchen manager all the way up to our executive team, directors, folks that are doing payroll, to . . . hear first hand what's going on."\par \par \uc2 Stephane Bancel\uc2 , vaccine maker \uc2 Moderna Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Working from Moderna's Massachusetts office\par \par \uc2 "We were literally working seven days a week nonstop. I had to make sure I was doing sport in order to stay healthy and to stay mentally sane."\par \par \uc2 Jean Hynes\uc2 , investment firm \uc2 Wellington Management Co\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Working from her home office in Massachusetts\par \par \uc2 "What we've heard back from our employees is that in increasing transparency we took away a lot of the stress. . . . We needed to be stress absorbers for the organization."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Chip Cutter and Kathryn Dill contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (See accompanying photos -- WSJ April 3, 2021)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210403eh430001d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
