\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Business News: With Remote Work, No One Knows When to Stop\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chip Cutter \par \uc2 826 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 March 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The daily alarm Katie Lipp sets isn't meant to wake her up. It reminds her to go to bed.\par \par \uc2 The employment attorney in Fairfax, Va., said she has tried a range of techniques to set boundaries while working long days from home running her law practice during the pandemic. Few measures work as well as the 9:45 p.m. alarm she started setting last month, though she admits to snoozing it occasionally to fire out one last email.\par \par \uc2 "You never feel like what you're doing is good enough, so you get stuck in a trap of overworking," said Ms. Lipp, the mother of a 5-year-old. "Sleep is the difference. If I get like eight to nine hours, I can take on the world. If I have six hours of sleep, it's like the walking dead."\par \par \uc2 A year into the Covid-19 era, many can relate. Employees say work-life boundaries blurred, then vanished, as waking life came to mean "always on" at work.\par \par \uc2 Experts warn that working around the clock -- while slipping in meals, helping with homework and grabbing a few moments with a partner -- isn't sustainable, and employers from banking giant \uc2 Citigroup Inc\uc2 . to the software company \uc2 Pegasystems Inc\uc2 ., are trying ways to get staff to dial back.\par \par \uc2 At consulting giant \uc2 Accenture PLC\uc2 , \uc2 Jimmy Etheredge\uc2 , the company's chief executive officer of North America, is embracing the notion of "taking back lunch," eating in peace away from screens and recharging in the middle of every workday.\par \par \uc2 The company is encouraging employees not to schedule internal meetings unrelated to client business on Fridays, and Mr. Etheredge has repeatedly told employees to be candid with managers, saying, "It's OK to not be OK."\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Slack Technologies Inc\uc2 ., the default meeting length on employee calendars is now 25 minutes, down from a half-hour, said Sheela Subramanian, a senior director.\par \par \uc2 She uses her five-minute breaks to check on her children, use the restroom or do jumping jacks.\par \par \uc2 In addition to minibreaks to refocus, Ms. Subramanian said she and many of her peers now employ a corporate version of college office hours. Her team views the core hours between 10 a.m. and 3 p.m. as fair game to meet online and collaborate. They arrange the rest of their workday as needed, she said, whether that means getting heads-down work done or dealing with home and family matters.\par \par \uc2 Though companies are trying to help their people set boundaries, employers also have largely been the beneficiaries of pandemic overwork, research has shown. An analysis of census and survey data published last fall found that Americans spent 60 million fewer hours commuting and spent much of those hours on additional work.\par \par \uc2 At German business-software maker \uc2 SAP SE\uc2 , which employs about 102,000 globally, more than 60% of staff surveyed recently said they felt they were working above their capacity.\par \par \uc2 The company has scheduled a global mental-health day for Tuesday, April 27, so everyone can take a day off. When it was announced, DJ Paoni, SAP's president of North America, said he received 450 emails from employees praising the move.\par \par \uc2 "If people are happy with their jobs, they're not out looking for other jobs and they're not leaving the company," he said. Even a 1% rise in employee turnover could cost SAP millions of dollars to hire and replace workers, he added.\par \par \uc2 Other companies are doing the same; Citi is taking May 28 as "Citi Reset Day," while \uc2 Alphabet Inc\uc2 .'s \uc2 Google\uc2  took a global day off on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Esther Perel, a therapist and host of the popular "How's Work?" podcast, has counseled workers virtually through the pandemic and observed that many have embraced all-consuming work, reasoning that burning out beats being unemployed.\par \par \uc2 While some people have feared job losses, others are bingeing on work to occupy their time, regain a sense of control and cope with a crisis that made them feel powerless, or to support loved ones out of work, Ms. Perel said.\par \par \uc2 "They kept their foot on the pedal," she said, making a sputtering sound. "It's like the motor is just completely overrun."\par \par \uc2 In Chicago, managers at consulting firm West Monroe monitor which employees consistently bill clients for more than 50 hours a week, a sign they may be overwhelmed. Managers will reach out and say, "Either you've got too much work to do, or your deadlines are too close, or we need to add another person to your team," said Kevin McCarty, West Monroe's CEO.\par \par \uc2 The goal is to make sure employees maintain reasonable hours and take vacation time, he said, and "people aren't wearing themselves out and saying, 'Well, I've got nothing else to do, so I'm going to work 15 hours a day for three months straight.'"\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210327eh3r0002c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
