\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Restaurant Chain Sets $10 Hourly Minimum\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matt Grossman \par \uc2 255 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 March 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Restaurant workers for the Olive Garden and LongHorn Steakhouse dining chains will soon be guaranteed a higher minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 Starting Monday, hourly restaurant workers at both chains will earn at least $10 an hour in wages and tips combined, with further increases to $12 an hour to be phased in through 2023.\par \par \uc2 The raise makes \uc2 Darden Restaurants Inc\uc2 ., the brands' Orlando, Fla.-based parent company, the latest business to institute a wage floor higher than the federally mandated minimum.\par \par \uc2 On average, hourly restaurant workers at Darden already earned more than $17 an hour in combined wages and tips, the company said. As of May 31, the company employed 167,000 hourly restaurant workers. During the height of the Covid-19 pandemic. Darden temporarily furloughed 150,000 people from its total workforce of 177,000, although a significant portion had been brought back before last summer.\par \par \uc2 Darden also said it would give hourly restaurant workers a bonus to reward their work during the coronavirus pandemic, setting aside $17 million for the one-time payments.\par \par \uc2 Labor activists have championed a $15-an-hour federal minimum wage for years, arguing that the current level, $7.25 an hour, hasn't kept pace with the cost of living. The last minimum-wage rise came in 2009.\par \par \uc2 Darden's third-quarter sales and profit remained hampered by the effects of the pandemic, but it said same-restaurant sales have exceeded pre-pandemic levels in the second half of March.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210326eh3q0000g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
