\par\par \b \uc2 Some Workers Yearn for Their Commutes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Berger \par \uc2 824 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 March 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A9A\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Kevin Corbett is president and chief executive officer of NJ Transit. An article on Friday about commuters incorrectly referred to him as the agency's executive director.\par \par \uc2 (WSJ March 29, 2021)\par \par \uc2 (END)\par \par \uc2 Jonathan White can't wait to get back to the office in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 Mr. White misses his 50-minute train ride from Westchester County to Grand Central Terminal that afforded time to read or listen to a podcast and calibrate for the day. He misses the separation of work and home life. Most of all, he misses impromptu conversations with colleagues and clients.\par \par \uc2 "Business is a contact sport," said Mr. White, global head of fund sales at \uc2 Intertrust Group\uc2 , a specialized fund administrator. "Doing it over screens isn't as good as doing it in person."\par \par \uc2 Mr. White aims to start commuting one day a week in April, and to travel more frequently in the following months. Many commuters in New York, New Jersey and Connecticut share his desire.\par \par \uc2 Officials at New York's \uc2 Metropolitan Transportation Authority\uc2 , which runs two commuter railroads and New York City's subways and buses, have noticed that ridership is growing faster on weekends than weekdays. Officials believe the trend indicates that many people feel comfortable boarding buses and trains again, and that the only thing holding them back midweek is the reopening of offices, said Abbey Collins, an MTA spokeswoman.\par \par \uc2 Riders surveyed in December by NJ Transit, New Jersey's statewide mass-transit system, found that between one-quarter and one-third of rail and bus customers who were working from home wanted to return to the workplace immediately, or within a few weeks.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Corbett\uc2 , NJ Transit's executive director, said he was surprised by the number of people eager to return to their commutes because the survey of 50,000 riders was conducted when Covid-19 cases were rising and before vaccines were widespread.\par \par \uc2 Such pent-up demand won't be enough to return ridership to pre-pandemic levels quickly. Many commuters remain reluctant to board buses and trains. Between about 15% and 20% of surveyed NJ Transit rail and bus riders currently working from home said they wouldn't be comfortable returning to the workplace at all. But the desire among some workers to go back to the office is a bright spot for transit officials who are running lots of half-empty vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Transit agencies, on average, rely on fares to cover one-third of their operating budget, according to the \uc2 American Public Transportation Association\uc2 . Transit officials in the region hope that as offices reopen this year, commuters return quickly.\par \par \uc2 One warning sign for agencies lies in the number of riders who expect to continue working from home, at least some of the time. Almost half of the surveyed NJ Transit rail and interstate bus riders who were working from home in December said they expected to ride less frequently after the pandemic, often because they expected to be working remotely.\par \par \uc2 Like many NJ Transit riders, Rich Rykowski, an IT director for a Manhattan fashion company, said he believes he will work from home one or two days a week when the pandemic is over. He started commuting one day a week from his home in Waldwick, N.J., in late February. His two-hour journey to Manhattan's Upper East Side involves two trains and a subway ride.\par \par \uc2 "I wouldn't say it's something I wanted," he said. "But it's definitely easier to get things done when you're in an office."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rykowski has spent most of the past year working from his kitchen table. He has three children, ages 2, 3 and 7, and three dogs. "People expect you to always be on camera," he said. "The kids are in the background screaming, climbing all over the place, the dogs are barking."\par \par \uc2 The slow recovery to pre-pandemic ridership levels poses problems for transit agencies, which have been relying on the federal government for tens of billions of dollars in relief. At some point, agencies will need to find new revenue streams or cut costs to balance budgets.\par \par \uc2 Train and bus ridership across the New York metropolitan region languishes at between 20% and 50% of pre-pandemic levels. Rider trends have highlighted the economic inequality between those who have been able to work from home and those who have been forced to travel during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 MTA\uc2  officials hope that, absent a new wave of infections, subway ridership could tick up in coming months. Many schools have reopened. Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2  said this week that tens of thousands of municipal workers will return to offices starting in May. A recent survey by the Partnership for New York City, a business group, found that almost half of the city's one million private-sector office workers are expected to return, at least part-time, by September.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210326eh3q0001k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
