\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Careers & Leadership: How to Tout Job Skills That Transfer\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kathryn Dill \par \uc2 820 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 March 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 By choice or necessity, many workers are seizing the moment to break into new industries.\par \par \uc2 For some, it's an imperative. Even as hiring picks up, the U.S. has 9.5 million fewer jobs than one year ago, before the pandemic. Industries battered by lockdowns, such as travel and hospitality, have struggled, driving many workers to look for jobs in other sectors. Some workers who have held on to their jobs or found new ones after a layoff feel stagnant or underemployed. Many hope an industry change will mean greater security.\par \par \uc2 Job-seekers intent on a career pivot need to persuade employers that their achievements in one industry can translate into wins in another. With a herd of applicants vying for each opening, now is not the time to expect hiring managers to make the connection between your past and your potential on their own.\par \par \uc2 "People are not going to notice your transferable skills unless you articulate them up front," Jane Oates, president of unemployment-focused nonprofit WorkingNation, said at The Wall Street Journal Job Summit on March 4. At the event, economists, executives and career coaches suggested how job seekers can leverage transferable skills. Here is their advice:\par \par \uc2 Evaluate your skills\par \par \uc2 Ms. Oates encourages job seekers to list their skills -- from communication abilities to proficiency in programming languages -- and practice describing them.\par \par \uc2 "You have to look at a day on your job and think about what are the things you routinely do," she said. "Everything. Things you do rotely are just as important as the things that are more defined and professional."\par \par \uc2 Don't overlook things that might seem too simple, like opening a store on time each day or knowing when a teammate needs help and you should pitch in.\par \par \uc2 Include experience using digital tools or platforms, which may demonstrate to potential employers that you could quickly learn the systems they rely on.\par \par \uc2 Ask someone who knows you well -- a colleague, friend or job coach -- to review your assessment. They can help you determine whether you have over- or under-sold your abilities, Ms. Oates said.\par \par \uc2 Don't focus on job titles\par \par \uc2 Considering different jobs and fields your skills could apply to can widen your search for roles you qualify for, according to LaFawn Davis, group vice president of environmental, social and governance at the job-search site Indeed.\par \par \uc2 "Gone are the days where you just look for an exact match of the title that you've had," said Ms. Davis. "It really is about searching for the right job for you, not just the typical job profile."\par \par \uc2 A bartender's skill set -- which might include communication and listening, efficiency, multitasking and the ability to move quickly between tasks -- could be applicable, for example, to a sales job, said Ms. Oates. Employees coming from public-facing occupations also might excel in customer service and call-center roles, which can be an entry to a new industry or career.\par \par \uc2 "Someone at a call center has to be able to respond to problems, and has to solve them quickly," she said, noting that many customer service employees have to become familiar with highly technical products and services.\par \par \uc2 Emphasize transferability in your resume\par \par \uc2 Paige Ross, senior managing director and global head of human resources, at private-equity firm \uc2 Blackstone\uc2 , tells job seekers looking to pivot to study a job description and see how the skills they have line up, then emphasize those connections for hiring managers.\par \par \uc2 "Build that into the resume -- 'by doing this, I learned how to do this,' " she said, adding that applicants should demonstrate their enthusiasm about the potential pivot in their cover letter. "Just writing, 'Oh, this looks interesting' won't get anybody's interest. But, 'I'm interested in making this switch because I'm passionate about doing this,' and really being specific about why you want to make the transition, it gets people's interests and their attention."\par \par \uc2 Scott Bonneau, vice president of global talent attraction at Indeed, suggests explaining the shift in an "objective" section at the top of a resume. "Describe why you think your experience doing work in one field is going to help you be a really strong performer in this new one that you're pivoting into," he said.\par \par \uc2 Sharpen and expand your skills\par \par \uc2 Ms. Oates recommends that workers who are employed but considering a change take every opportunity their current employer provides to learn something new or earn a credential.\par \par \uc2 She cautions that workers should frame any discussion about professional development in terms of the potential to advance within the company.\par \par \uc2 Consider opening the conversation, she advises, by saying something like, "I want to build my professional life here, but I'd like to do something a little bit different and I'd like to be considered for new jobs as they come up."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210322eh3m0000v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
