\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: High Court to Hear Farmworkers Organizing Case\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jess Bravin \par \uc2 666 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 March 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- One of labor leader Cesar Chavez's proudest achievements faces scrutiny Monday at the Supreme Court, where growers are challenging the rights that union organizers hold under California law to make their case to farmworkers in the fields.\par \par \uc2 That access regulation has been part of California law since 1975, when the state Legislature, declaring its intention "to ensure peace in the agricultural fields by guaranteeing justice for all agricultural workers and stability in labor relations," granted collective-bargaining rights to farmworkers. That was a change Mr. Chavez and his United Farm Workers union had sought. The California Supreme Court upheld the access regulation over a grower challenge, and in 1976 the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  declined to hear their appeal.\par \par \uc2 California growers are now arguing that the regulation, which allows organizers to enter their property to meet with workers during lunch hour and for one hour before and after their shifts, is a relic of the pre-digital era. Unions nowadays can organize through other means, they say, such as social media, text messages and off-site encounters.\par \par \uc2 The case has been filed by the conservative Pacific Legal Foundation on behalf of two growers that have fought UFW organizers, Cedar Point Nursery, which raises strawberry plants in Dorris, Calif., and Fresno-based Fowler Packing Co., a major citrus and table-grape producer.\par \par \uc2 "My family works in the business, we treat [workers] like we treat our kids," Cedar Point owner Mike Fahner said at a Pacific Legal press briefing. "The need for a union? It's certainly unclear to me," he said.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit argues that the regulation seizes from growers a basic element of private property: the right to exclude others. That makes it a "taking" of private property for public use, triggering the state's constitutional obligation to pay the growers just compensation, an argument that failed in the lower courts.\par \par \uc2 One thing that hasn't changed since the 1970s is the nature of agricultural labor. It remains grueling, low-paid work and often requires frequent relocations to follow the seasons of different crops. California argues that union access is the only way many farmworkers can learn their rights because they are frequently poor, lack basic education and have limited access to the technology that has eased communication for others.\par \par \uc2 "It's an incredibly challenging population to reach," says Victoria Hassid, chairwoman of the California Agricultural Labor Relations Board. Workers often are transported by labor contractors to different locations daily on expansive farmlands, themselves in isolated rural locations. Organizers can't stand by a subway stop or on the sidewalk near a building entrance to distribute leaflets. "There's no breakroom for these workers, there's no centralized parking lot," she said.\par \par \uc2 The state disputes that its regulation is a taking at all, because organizers' access is limited and they face penalties if they disrupt the workplace. Unions must serve notice to the grower of a visit and file a copy with the labor board, beginning a 30-day access period. Up to four such periods are permitted a year, but access ends after an organizing election and can't be sought again for another year.\par \par \uc2 The access right has been used sparingly. There are some 16,000 agricultural employers in California, the state says, but typically only a few dozen access notices are filed with the labor board each year.\par \par \uc2 "It's used very occasionally, and it's used where there is definitely interest among the workers in receiving more information" about the union, said Mario Martinez, the UFW's general counsel.\par \par \uc2 According to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , less than 2% of the nation's 1.3 million agricultural workers were represented by a union in 2020, among the lowest of any industrial sector. In a 2019 government filing, the UFW said it had 7,500 members -- less than one-tenth the number it claimed in the early 1970s.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210322eh3m0001c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
