\par\par \b \uc2 Work From Home (A Special Report) --- Listen to Music While Working? It Depends: A study suggests it can sometimes help performance\b0\par\par\uc2 By Heidi Mitchell \par \uc2 633 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 March 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Should you listen to music when you're working from home?\par \par \uc2 According to psychology researchers who have delved into the details of when music helps performance and when it hurts, there are three questions you should ask yourself: What is the music? What is the task? And do you like having distractions around?\par \par \uc2 Manuel F. Gonzalez, a postdoctoral researcher in the department of psychology at \uc2 Baruch College\uc2 , and John R. "Jack" Aiello, a professor in the department of psychology at \uc2 Rutgers, the State University of New Jersey\uc2 , published a paper in the Journal of Experimental Psychology in September 2019 on how performance is affected by music. The study has taken on additional relevance with so many people working from home during the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The two men recruited 150 undergraduate students to participate individually in a lab experiment. The researchers first assessed the students' baseline cognitive abilities so they could rule out intelligence as an alternative explanation of their findings. Then they had the diverse group of students fill out surveys about boredom proneness, including their preferences for external stimulation. Finally, they asked participants to complete a simple cognitive task of crossing off words on a list that had the letter A in them, and a complex task, which required the students to memorize word pairs and then recall which word belonged with which partner when prompted.\par \par \uc2 Some participants performed both tasks in silence, while others did them while listening to an original musical composition. Some of those listening to music heard a simple composition, while others heard a more complex version of the same tune.\par \par \uc2 The psychologists unearthed three takeaways.\par \par \uc2 For the complex, word-pairing task, participants scored about 4% lower on average when they listened to any music, compared with those who completed the task in silence. "That is consistent with distraction conflict theory, which basically states that there are only so many resources we have to work with, and if those resources are heavily invested in a task, there is not a lot left over," says Dr. Aiello. "And if you add potential distractions, like music, the task will be even more difficult."\par \par \uc2 But in completing the simple task, participants scored about 7% higher on average when they listened to complex music, compared with those who either listened to simple music or no music. "If you have a simple task, you have more resources left over, and the music can focus your mind," says Dr. Gonzalez.\par \par \uc2 However, the researchers found that people who are prone to seek distractions out of boredom (think social media, dirty dishes, children coming to and fro, smartphones pinging) tend to do worse if they listen to music while performing a task -- simple or complex.\par \par \uc2 "We suspect that even though listening to complex music might be beneficial for some, it can be overly distracting for people who are prone to heavily engage with their environment, because they may become too absorbed in the music," says Mr. Gonzalez.\par \par \uc2 For people working at home because of the pandemic, "the beauty and the curse are that we are the masters of our own distraction," says Dr. Gonzalez. Learning how to manage distractions "requires some self-awareness, particularly in terms of what we experience as simple or complex work, and how stimulating we're making our environment through music. It's kind of like pairing the right wine with a meal," he says. "But maybe we are all learning the best way to work efficiently in any scenario and that can be applied when some of us return to the office."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ms. Mitchell is a writer in Chicago. Email her at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210322eh3m00002\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
