\par\par \b \uc2 Work From Home (A Special Report) --- The Pets We're Leaving Behind: Dogs and cats may feel stressed when their owners return to the office\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bruce Horovitz \par \uc2 514 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 March 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Many workers are eager to return to the office, at least some of the time. But what about the pets they will be leaving behind?\par \par \uc2 For thousands of cats and dogs adopted during the pandemic, this will be the first time they'll regularly be left in their new home alone for extended periods. And even for those whose owners worked outside the home before the pandemic, this will be another major disruption.\par \par \uc2 So, what should pet owners do to prepare their pets for a home that suddenly will be without people for some or all of the workday?\par \par \uc2 For answers, we reached out to Andrea Y. Tu, a veterinarian pet-behavior specialist and medical director at Behavior Vets in New York City. Edited excerpts follow:\par \par \uc2 WSJ: How might we be stressing our pets when we return to work?\par \par \uc2 DR. TU: Any change in routine can be very stressful for a pet. They've been used to having you at home for the past year. When you go back to work, it's yet another change, and that's stressful. Even pets that are stressed with you at home have the potential to be stressed by the change of you returning to work.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: What types of pets are likely to experience the most stress by their owners returning to work?\par \par \uc2 DR. TU: Puppies and kittens purchased during the quarantine. They have basically never experienced not having a human at home 24 hours a day, seven days a week.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: How do we help our pets transition to our new work schedules?\par \par \uc2 DR. TU: You need to try to stick with the same schedule every day, if possible. Try to keep the same times when they are fed, walked, and get in their big playtime. So even when you're still working from home, try to stick with the schedule you'll be using once you're back at work.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: What other things can we do to prepare our pets to be alone?\par \par \uc2 DR. TU: Start training independence while you're still home. You can place them, or possibly crate them, in a different room for several hours. You can also just shut the door to the room you're working in for, say, three hours a day.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: How do we keep our pets from getting bored or destroying the house when we return to work?\par \par \uc2 DR. TU: You want to give them something to do. You want to get them used to being able to keep themselves entertained. One way to do this is with a simple puzzle toy, like a Kong. No dog or cat should be fed out of a bowl. That is a lost opportunity for engagement. A treat-dispensing toy makes the mealtime long, more fun and much closer to normal animal behavior.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mr. Horovitz is a writer in Falls Church, Va. Email him at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210322eh3m0000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
