\par\par \b \uc2 Work From Home (A Special Report) --- When Working From Home, What Exactly Is a Sick Day? Many people aren't taking time off, forcing companies to adjust\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alina Dizik \par \uc2 711 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 March 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Since the start of the pandemic, Nyala Khan, head of human resources at a health startup, has been on a mission to encourage employees to take sick days. But they aren't always listening.\par \par \uc2 Since the company's employees are currently working from home, "a lot of people will say, 'I'm not feeling well, but will work on a few things throughout the day,' " says Ms. Khan, head of talent at New York's Eden Health. Last year, when the company began tracking personal time off, roughly 52%, or 1,362 hours, went unused, she adds.\par \par \uc2 The company is reminding people to take staycations or vacations this year for personal well-being, with Ms. Khan personally doing presentations of inspiring places to visit, she says.\par \par \uc2 At many companies like Ms. Khan's, the pandemic and widespread remote work are changing the nature of sick days. Because people can already work at their own pace, many aren't taking sick days at all -- they just plow ahead with tasks on a schedule that suits them. Even when people do take sick days, they are increasingly inclined to work anyway.\par \par \uc2 Companies, meanwhile, are struggling to figure out what to do about this new reality. Some are offering more sick time, for a wider range of reasons -- including mental health -- but there is no consensus about how to get employees to take it. Or how to get them to really relax when they do.\par \par \uc2 Studies show that working while sick increased the risk of sick leave later on and is likely to affect your mental health. Another Danish study found that workers who went to work ill were more likely to later experience depression. "In general, working while sick does negatively affect well-being," says Alison Collins, who researches presenteeism, or working while sick, at \uc2 Manchester Metropolitan University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some researchers, though, think there may be an upside to soldiering on during the pandemic. That's because during the pandemic, people who feel ill may actually find more enjoyment from doing some work rather than simply being bored at home with few distractions, says Wladislaw Rivkin, an organizational behavior professor at Trinity College Dublin, who researches presenteeism.\par \par \uc2 Many companies, meanwhile, are rethinking their policies on sick leave. Roughly one in five companies has made changes to their personal time off and vacation rules due to the pandemic, according to data from June 2020 from the \uc2 Society for Human Resource Management\uc2 , a trade group.\par \par \uc2 At least one big change is being imposed on them: As a result of the pandemic, federal guidelines mandated two weeks of paid sick leave at many organizations if related to Covid-19. Some larger firms haven't only increased their number of sick days to adhere to regulations but have also started tracking days more diligently to better understand how employees use them, says Lisa Frydenlund, knowledge adviser at the society.\par \par \uc2 Some companies, for instance, are once again separating sick days into their own category to track them more thoroughly, a policy that was decreasing for the past decade.\par \par \uc2 Other firms have vowed a more hands-off approach. After seeing how productive their employees can be outside of the office, some have stopped keeping track of vacation and sick days altogether, says Prithwiraj Choudhury, a Harvard Business School professor who studies work-from-anywhere setups. "Part of this philosophy is, 'Let's give people flexibility and hope that people will be responsible,' " he says.\par \par \uc2 As more people start to return to the office, workers can expect the expansive new view of sick time to stay, says \uc2 Nicholas Bloom\uc2 , professor of economics at Stanford University. Nearly a year later, many employees already feel more comfortable calling in sick when working in-person and are less hesitant to take the opportunity to work from home. "If you're sick and coming up to that key promotion, you would have had to come in. Post-pandemic, you are still going to be working from home," he says.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ms. Dizik is a writer in Chicago. She can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210322eh3m0000c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
