\par\par \b \uc2 Work From Home (A Special Report) --- New Terms of Engagement: Many people have felt disengaged from work during Covid. Blame the boss.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Francesca Gino and Daniel Cable \par \uc2 937 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 March 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Call it a pandemic of worker malaise: Thanks to Covid-19, an increasing number of people feel disengaged from their jobs. Working from home, interacting via video, and worrying about our health and the economy are all taking a toll. In a May-June 2020 survey of over 8,000 people across all 50 states and all types of jobs, we found that work engagement had fallen 16% since the start of the pandemic. We replicated those findings in a subsequent survey of over 500 people in February.\par \par \uc2 There was, however, one factor that protected people against work disengagement: their crisis mind-set. Many of our respondents viewed the pandemic as a threat and an annoyance, and just wanted life to go back to normal. These were the disengaged workers. But others were trying to make the most of the changing world. Our survey showed that these people experienced almost no drop in work engagement. In fact, some even became more energized by their work during this period.\par \par \uc2 We might assume that whether people approach the upheaval of the pandemic as an opportunity or a threat depends wholly on them -- their personality, life experiences and emotions. In our research, however, we uncovered an interesting truth: Bosses made a big difference.\par \par \uc2 In a subsequent survey of over 500 people, we identified exactly how leaders are making things worse, as well as how they can help improve the situation.\par \par \uc2 -- Leaders as role models.\par \par \uc2 The biggest issue, by far, was how leaders themselves responded to the crisis. People in our online executive M.B.A. classes often talk about how their leaders "can't wait to go back to how things were." One senior leader told us before a speech: "Don't say too many good things about working from home, because I want them back into the office as soon as possible."\par \par \uc2 These leaders are modeling a negative crisis mind-set -- and passing it on to their subordinates. Research on emotional contagion confirms that we can "catch" each other's emotions. Think about times when a friend's "infectious" good mood cheered you up, or when someone's unhappiness left you feeling low.\par \par \uc2 Our survey showed that bosses' attitudes were the single best predictor of employees' crisis mind-sets, and thus their work engagement. When bosses were "just trying to get through this period," they ingrained a threat mind-set that decimated their employees' work engagement. By contrast, the employees of leaders who were "focused on the opportunity this crisis provided" had the highest opportunity mind-sets in our survey.\par \par \uc2 An opportunity crisis mind-set isn't the same as sunny optimism that somehow everything will be OK. Rather, during the pandemic, leaders who have balanced realism and optimism signal to employees the value of experimenting and building skills that will be valuable both during and after the crisis. They encourage employees to reflect on opportunities to rethink their jobs during this period of reset, recognizing that work becomes more engaging when we are guided by our personal interests, perspectives and talents.\par \par \uc2 -- Get personal.\par \par \uc2 In our survey, a second important predictor of an employee's opportunity mind-set was whether "my boss has had conversations with me about my home life in addition to my work life."\par \par \uc2 Many bosses have avoided asking employees about their home life. It makes sense: They've long been trained to "be professional" and keep emotions out of the workplace.\par \par \uc2 During normal times, this detached style of leadership does little to promote employee engagement; workers are reluctant to experiment and learn from leaders they don't connect with or trust. It's even more damaging in a pandemic, when employees are dealing with immense burdens that can leave them feeling isolated, overburdened and taken for granted.\par \par \uc2 It turns out that when leaders use the pandemic to connect with employees as human beings, their employees are significantly more likely to view lockdown as a time to learn and engage.\par \par \uc2 -- See the big picture.\par \par \uc2 In our survey, talking about the purpose of the work to be done was the third most important behavior leaders could engage in to encourage an opportunity mind-set in their employees.\par \par \uc2 Leaders have had to put out a lot of fires these days -- overseeing new online systems, finding new ways to service customers, overcoming supply-chain challenges, managing employees from a distance, among other things. This focus on the details is necessary, but often has detracted from broader concerns.\par \par \uc2 While working from home, many of us have struggled with some basic questions: "What is my job now?" and "How do I help my organization succeed?" When leaders talk with employees about their goals and the impact they can make together, they help employees see how their work fits into the bigger picture.\par \par \uc2 More broadly, it's a reminder that the little things have the broadest impact. Workers have felt less and less engaged this past year. Many bosses, thinking they were doing the right thing, have only made things worse. But it isn't too late for all of us to improve.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ms. Gino is a Harvard Business School professor and the author of "Rebel Talent: Why It Pays to Break the Rules at Work and in Life." Mr. Cable is a professor of organizational behavior at \uc2 London Business School\uc2  and the author of "Exceptional: Build Your Personal Highlight Reel and Unlock Your Potential." They can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw286\pich640\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4290\pichgoal9600
