\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Business News: Companies Wrestle With Hybrid Work Plans\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emily Glazer and Chip Cutter \par \uc2 778 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 March 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Big U.S. companies are discovering that "hybrid" work comes with plenty of complications.\par \par \uc2 As employers firm up plans to bring white-collar workers back into offices while still allowing them to do some work at home, many are encountering obstacles. Companies are grappling with what new schedules employees should follow, where people should sit in redesigned offices and how best to prevent employees at home from feeling left out of impromptu discussions or being passed over for opportunities, say chief executives, board directors and others.\par \par \uc2 Insurer Prudential Financial Inc., which expects most of its roughly 42,000 employees to work in the office half the time starting after Labor Day, wants to make certain not all staffers choose to stay home Mondays and Fridays and then work in the office midweek. At travel company \uc2 Expedia Group Inc\uc2 ., executives are trying to figure out how to have in-person meetings that don't disadvantage those who aren't in the room. Other employers, including software company \uc2 Twilio Inc\uc2 ., predict the new era of work could lead to shuffling between teams, with staffers gravitating to bosses who embrace their preferred styles of working.\par \par \uc2 Hybrid work "is going to redefine expectations, rules, permissions," says Kevin McCarty, chief executive officer of Chicago-based consulting firm West Monroe, which employs 1,360 people, and is rethinking when its employees should work at home or come into its offices.\par \par \uc2 Though executives say it would be easier to manage if every employee returned to an office, or all stayed remote, surveys have repeatedly shown that most workers want a mixed approach as more adults are vaccinated. In a February survey of 1,000 companies commissioned by LaSalle Network, a national staffing and recruiting firm, the majority of companies said they would adopt a hybrid model.\par \par \uc2 Companies have also polled their organizations to find out how employees feel. At \uc2 Prudential\uc2 , most employees indicated that they enjoyed working remotely but missed the planning, ideation and collaboration that takes place in person, says \uc2 Rob Falzon\uc2 , vice chair of the company.\par \par \uc2 Prudential\uc2  has been redesigning its office space and repurposing most of it for meeting rooms, collaboration and open space so people will be more likely to interact.\par \par \uc2 Like many employers, the company is reducing its physical footprint so there won't be available desks for people who want to go to the office more frequently, with exceptions for some employees including traders. "We don't have a desk for you every day," Mr. Falzon says. "We have a desk for you three days a week."\par \par \uc2 Hybrid models range by company. Technology company \uc2 Adobe Inc\uc2 . plans to allow employees to work from home up to two to three days a week, with staffers able to make reservations for office desks, says \uc2 Gloria Chen\uc2 , the company's chief people officer. Other companies are hesitant to put a specific number on days allowed at home.\par \par \uc2 "We won't prescribe" from a company level, says \uc2 David Henshall\uc2 , CEO at technology company \uc2 Citrix Systems Inc\uc2 . "Based on the type of role you have, you'll find that right balance."\par \par \uc2 With flexibility can come challenges. If a team comes together in person, but not all can make it, that potentially creates a subpar experience for those not in the room, says Expedia CEO \uc2 Peter Kern\uc2 . The travel company opened the first phases of an expansive campus -- complete with Wi-Fi-equipped rocks -- on the shores of Seattle's Elliott Bay before the pandemic, and plans to initially permit spaced group team meetings at its headquarters.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kern, though, says he has questions about whether those on Zoom will get the same level of learning, encouragement and career growth as those in the room. Then there are the scheduling issues.\par \par \uc2 Managers may need to "set up group meetings according to some crazy algorithm of: Who's available when? Who's got a flexible day, when?" Mr. Kern says. "There's a lot of friction in all of that. It's a lot easier to say, 'Everybody go to work.' Now someone calls a meeting, and you're all there."\par \par \uc2 Some human-resources professionals say companies have little choice but to accommodate workers' demands, as an inflexible workplace could drive employees away as the economy rebounds, and because many workers have proven themselves adept at working anywhere.\par \par \uc2 "The employer before just could say, 'Our culture is this,'" says \uc2 Tara Wolckenhauer\uc2 , a human-resources executive at payroll processor \uc2 Automatic Data Processing Inc\uc2 . "Employers have to take a step back and think about it very differently."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Krithika Varagur contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210320eh3k0002b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
