\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Sanders Targets CEO Pay as Part of Pro-Worker Plank\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eliza Collins and Richard Rubin \par \uc2 546 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 March 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Sen. Bernie Sanders is using his new perch as Budget Committee chairman to try to keep Congress focused on measures designed to improve worker pay and job conditions, even as related legislation including a $15-an-hour minimum wage has stalled.\par \par \uc2 The Vermont senator introduced legislation Wednesday that would seek to apply an additional tax on corporations where the CEO is paid more than 50 times the median worker. The proposal is among a handful of policies that the progressive ran on during his 2020 Democratic presidential campaign and is now pushing from atop the committee, where he can schedule hearings and call witnesses to testify.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sanders's panel also held a hearing Wednesday focused on wealth and income inequality, as well as workers' ability to unionize. The session included testimony from an \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . worker trying to unionize at a fulfillment center in Alabama. The senator said he plans to hold another hearing this month on corporations' tax obligations.\par \par \uc2 "I intend to talk about the most important issues facing working families," Mr. Sanders said in an interview Tuesday, adding that some of the measures he pursues as chairman overlap with those he pushed during his two presidential campaigns. "Right now, I happen to believe that this country is on its way to an oligarchy."\par \par \uc2 Some of his ideas face criticism from both centrist Democrats and Republicans, who say his proposals are unrealistic and costly. Republicans say higher corporate tax rates pursued by Democrats and a $15 minimum wage would hurt companies and could ultimately force them to hire fewer employees. A \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  report on the impact of a $15 minimum wage found it would lift many workers out of poverty but also cost jobs.\par \par \uc2 Sen. Pat Toomey\uc2  (R., Pa.), a member of the budget committee, said at the hearing Wednesday that the government didn't need to take drastic measures, pointing to data showing wages rising.\par \par \uc2 "I know some would like to suggest we have a crisis, so as to justify various socialistic policies and even more redistribution of wealth, but the fact is the income gap has been narrowing and it's been narrowing at an accelerating pace," he said.\par \par \uc2 Since taking over the budget committee, Mr. Sanders has helped craft the $1.9 trillion coronavirus relief package and worked to include a provision for the higher wage that was ultimately dropped. Mr. Sanders, an independent who caucuses with the Democrats, also backs an infrastructure and jobs package that focuses on curbing climate change and has been in frequent touch with President Biden.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has backed a higher corporate tax rate, a minimum tax on profitable companies and tougher tax rules on foreign profits. All face uncertainty in Congress. Mr. Sanders's latest proposal -- tying a company's corporate tax rate to its compensation policies -- goes further. He says he hopes to build public support and then use that to pressure Democrats to back it.\par \par \uc2 "I am a grass-roots type of guy," Mr. Sanders said. Three Senate Democrats are co-sponsoring the bill with him; a companion bill set to be introduced in the House has 20 co-sponsors.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210318eh3i0000y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
