\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Home Offices Bring Shift To Corporate IT Strategies\b0\par\par\uc2 By Angus Loten \par \uc2 905 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 March 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 One year ago today, many companies in the U.S. emptied offices, shut down workplaces and sent employees home, after the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  declared the novel coronavirus a pandemic and brought economies around the world to a grinding halt.\par \par \uc2 Most businesses scrapped earlier plans and shifted into survival mode, turning primarily to cloud computing and remote-work software to weather lockdowns, travel bans and other disruptions, chief information officers and industry analysts say.\par \par \uc2 Overall, global IT spending declined 3.2% last year from 2019 levels, to $3.69 trillion, as companies "prioritized spending on technology and services that were deemed mission-critical during the initial stages of the pandemic," tech research and consulting firm \uc2 Gartner Inc\uc2  . said in a January report.\par \par \uc2 That's a sharp contrast to January last year, when \uc2 Gartner\uc2  forecast a 3.4% increase in world-wide IT spending by December in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Here is a look at the enterprise IT market under Covid-19.\par \par \uc2 Year of the Cloud\par \par \uc2 When the pandemic struck, companies quickly learned that transplanting IT systems from in-house servers to cloud providers made it easier to manage them from afar. It also gave employees readier access to business applications from home, in the form of cloud-based software.\par \par \uc2 "The race to digital business, enabled by cloud tech, was certainly at the heart of nearly everything done in 2020," said John-David Lovelock, vice president of research at \uc2 Gartner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While sales of computer servers to businesses across the economy dropped 3.1%, sales to cloud providers, such as \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2  .'s \uc2 Amazon Web Services\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft Corp\uc2  .'s Azure, grew 8.5%, Mr. Lovelock said.\par \par \uc2 Companies world-wide spent a record $34.6 billion on cloud services in the second quarter, up roughly 11% from the previous quarter and 30% from the same period in 2019, according to research firm Canalys.\par \par \uc2 "Within just the few weeks of stay-at-home advisories last spring, our sales volume increased rapidly across two continents," as more consumers began shopping at home, said \uc2 Jim Miller\uc2  , chief technology officer at \uc2 Wayfair Inc\uc2 . The online home-decorating retailer had shifted IT systems to \uc2 Google\uc2  Cloud in January.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Miller said the cloud enabled the company to handle the surge in demand. "We were able to take more or less a business-as-usual approach, even during prolonged periods of peak demand that would have lasted only a few days at a time pre-Covid," Mr. Miller said.\par \par \uc2 The Home Office\par \par \uc2 "Ultimately, the Covid year was all about making sure that employees could get their work done," said J.P. Gownder, vice president and principal analyst at Forrester Inc., an IT research firm. Yet data compiled by \uc2 Forrester\uc2  in May 2020 found only about 40% of global companies felt their IT infrastructure was prepared to handle remote work.\par \par \uc2 That prompted a run on videoconferencing software, among other remote-work and collaboration tools, with most deployments conducted in "fire-drill fashion," Mr. Gownder said. Videoconferencing platforms, like \uc2 Zoom Video Communications Inc\uc2  ., \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  Teams and \uc2 Cisco Systems Inc\uc2  .'s Webex, saw a surge in business, he said.\par \par \uc2 Gartner\uc2  estimates that more than 1.1 billion workers around the world worked remotely last year, up from 350 million in 2019.\par \par \uc2 In the last week of April, Zoom reported that the number of daily users on its platform grew to more than 300 million, up from 10 million at the end of 2019.\par \par \uc2 "The adoption of technology is the highest I've ever seen, and the rate is faster than I've ever seen," said \uc2 Cisco\uc2 's Chief Information Officer, Jacqui Guichelaar.\par \par \uc2 What the crisis taught companies, she said, is that work is "a thing you do, not a place you go." More than half of the employers she has met with over the past few months say they expect employees to work from home at least eight days every month, Ms. Guichelaar said.\par \par \uc2 Wayne Kurtzman\uc2 , a research director at \uc2 International Data Corp\uc2 ., said the crisis has accelerated the adoption of videoconferencing and other collaboration tools by roughly five years.\par \par \uc2 It has also driven innovation. New features expected in the year ahead include the use of artificial intelligence to enable real-time transcription and translation, informing people when they were mentioned in a meeting and why, and creating a short "greatest hits" version of meetings they may have missed, Mr. Kurtzman said.\par \par \uc2 Many businesses also ramped up their use of software bots, among other forms of automation, to handle routine workplace tasks like data entry and invoice processing.\par \par \uc2 Holding Pattern\par \par \uc2 The attention focused on keeping operations running saw many companies pull back on some long-running IT modernization efforts, or plans to build out ambitious data analytics and business intelligence systems.\par \par \uc2 Bob Parker\uc2  , a senior vice president for industry research at \uc2 IDC\uc2 , said many companies were simply channeling funds to more urgent needs. But another key obstacle was an inability to access on-site resources to continue pre-Covid initiatives, he said, "especially for projects requiring significant process re-engineering," such as enterprise resource planning implementations and upgrades.\par \par \uc2 That is likely to change in the months ahead, as the economy opens up and companies play catch-up with IT spending plans, analysts said. \uc2 Gartner\uc2  expects world-wide IT spending to jump 6.2% from 2020, reaching $3.9 trillion by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210315eh3f0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
