\par\par \b \uc2 Big Companies Get Go-Ahead To Give Vaccinations to Staff\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Krouse \par \uc2 992 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Large employers, from the meatpacking industry to pharmaceutical companies, are getting permission from public-health officials to administer Covid-19 vaccines, hoping to speed up inoculations of their employees.\par \par \uc2 Many businesses see giving vaccine doses to employees at work as a way to efficiently vaccinate staff but, in doing so, are joining a race for scarce shots.\par \par \uc2 Pharmaceutical company \uc2 AbbVie Inc\uc2 . has begun giving staff at its North Chicago headquarters doses, according to people familiar with the matter, with priority to those over 65 years old and then workers in operations and manufacturing. \uc2 Abbott Laboratories\uc2  also has begun giving doses at its nearby headquarters to eligible workers, such as those in manufacturing, food service and daycare, a spokeswoman said, and \uc2 Tyson Foods Inc\uc2 . has delivered doses to staff at its Joslin, Ill., beef plant and to some workers in Iowa, a spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Other companies registered to provide doses include energy giant \uc2 Exxon Mobil Corp\uc2 ., meatpacker \uc2 Smithfield Foods Inc\uc2 . and machinery-makers \uc2 Caterpillar Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Deere & Co\uc2 ., according to Illinois public-health records. Some of those companies run or are planning to run closed vaccine-giving events, meaning only their own staff are eligible, not the broader public. Sites are reliant on state and local public-health authorities for allocations of doses.\par \par \uc2 Money manager Fidelity Investments has registered to provide doses at its Boston headquarters and will begin giving shots to workers who are over 65 when it receives vaccines from Massachusetts, a spokesman said. A third-party health-and-wellness company will give the shots according to the state's prioritization guidelines, he added.\par \par \uc2 Throughout the pandemic, companies have jostled for access to safety-related tools, such as protective gear and testing capacity to protect workers and give customers and staff more confidence in shared spaces. Now their focus has shifted to vaccines.\par \par \uc2 Vaccine prioritization differs from state to state. In some jurisdictions, who is eligible for doses depends purely on age. In others, any worker in a prioritized sector, from healthcare to manufacturing, can get a dose regardless of whether their role involves interacting with the public, the ability to work from home or remotely, or the type of product they work on.\par \par \uc2 Companies that want to give shots to their workers typically have to register with public-health programs that approve who is eligible to receive allocations of Covid-19 vaccines. In general, the federal government allots doses to states, territories, a few large cities and some federal agencies, who then divvy them up among constituents or local health authorities.\par \par \uc2 Some healthcare-equity researchers said state prioritization guidelines can be overly broad, and risk having vaccine doses given to people who aren't at a high risk of contracting Covid-19 at work when supply remains constrained.\par \par \uc2 Vaccination in the workplace helps remove transit and time-off challenges for hourly workers, and it efficiently gets doses to those in high-risk positions, such as those where social distancing isn't possible, said Dr. Janice Bowie, professor in the department of health, behavior and society at the \uc2 Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health\uc2 . It also highlights a problem with classifying entire sectors as essential, when workers' roles, on-the-job risks and health conditions vary, she said.\par \par \uc2 "This is certainly not black or white" from an ethical perspective, given the current limited supply of vaccines nationally, Dr. Bowie said about businesses receiving doses to give to staff.\par \par \uc2 Some healthcare-equity experts said vaccine administration by employers can help speed up distribution because it takes eligible workers out of line at public sites and eases the appointment-making process. The challenge is that not all companies seek or are granted dose allocations, they said.\par \par \uc2 "It's a balancing act," Mark Pfister, executive director of the Lake County Health Department in Illinois, said of allocations to dose administrators in his jurisdiction, which include Abbott and \uc2 AbbVie\uc2 . Vaccine supply has increased, but many more entities now want doses, he said. His department asks companies to give priority to workers who are 65 and older, working close together or living in ZIP Codes hardest hit by Covid-19.\par \par \uc2 Providing on-site doses gives employers better visibility into who has received shots than if workers traveled to publicly run facilities, corporate medical advisers said. It also saves employers missed hours if workers have to travel to vaccine-administration sites during business hours and saves staff the cost of lost wages, child care and transit. "Employers have found this is the best way to get your population back to work as safely as possible," said Tobias Barker, chief medical officer at Everside Health, which assists employers with vaccination events.\par \par \uc2 An Abbott spokeswoman said the company is working with public-health officials in places where it has manufacturing facilities to offer vaccines to eligible workers when doses are available. Any vaccine doses the company receives go only to employees who meet government requirements for initial vaccine phases, she said.\par \par \uc2 A Deere & Co. spokeswoman said vaccines for its employees began last week at its five Illinois locations. It will make doses available to production and maintenance employees at its manufacturing units, and for salaried employees who consistently reported to its factories or offices since March 2020, which is a minority of such staff.\par \par \uc2 Keira Lombardo\uc2 , chief administrative officer at Smithfield, said the company and its partners can facilitate rapid distribution of vaccines to food and agriculture workers and is doing so based on state-specific guidelines. The company is prepared to help with distribution to workers in other essential categories, she added.\par \par \uc2 An Exxon spokesman said doses would be given according to local health-authority requirements. A spokeswoman for Caterpillar declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Airlines including \uc2 United Airlines Holdings Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 American Airlines Group Inc\uc2 . started giving doses of the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine last week to certain staff at Chicago's O'Hare International Airport.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210308eh3800029\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
