\par\par \b \uc2 House Set for Vote on Aid Bill After Narrow Senate Approval\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ted Mann and Kristina Peterson \par \uc2 1,133 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The $1.9 trillion Covid-19 relief bill faces what is expected to be a very close vote in the House as early as Tuesday after the Senate narrowly approved the aid package over the weekend with concessions to accommodate centrist Democrats.\par \par \uc2 The Senate changes to the bill, which first passed the House Feb. 27 with more generous unemployment provisions, mean House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  must hold together her slim majority caucus for a second House vote to send President Biden's top legislative priority to his desk.\par \par \uc2 The House is expected to hold a procedural vote on the bill Monday night, with final passage slated for Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 There were positive signals for Mrs. Pelosi (D., Calif.) and her leadership team over the weekend, when key progressives in the House appeared poised to support the relief bill a second time even as many expressed frustration at the changes that curtailed the extension of unemployment benefits and blocked an effort to gradually raise the federal minimum wage to $15 an hour.\par \par \uc2 "Despite the fact that we believe any weakening of the House provisions were bad policy and bad politics, the reality is that the final amendments were relatively minor concessions," said \uc2 Rep. Pramila Jayapal\uc2  (D., Wash.), who leads the Congressional Progressive Caucus.\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Pelosi can't afford many defections. The House version of the relief plan passed last month by a margin of 219-212, with two Democrats voting against it. The two no votes came from moderate Reps. Jared Golden of Maine and \uc2 Kurt Schrader\uc2  of Oregon.\par \par \uc2 All Republicans in the Senate, like all the GOP House members, voted against the legislation, balking at the price tag and saying some of the spending items weren't sufficiently geared toward the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 "This was not really about coronavirus in terms of the spending," said \uc2 Sen. John Barrasso\uc2  (R., Wyo.) on NBC on Sunday. "This was a liberal wish list of liberal spending, just basically filled with pork."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Jayapal said the bill represented about 7% of U.S. gross domestic product, in line with what some economists believed would be needed to help the economy recover from the coronavirus pandemic. And she noted the lingering rift within the Democratic ranks about the failure by the Senate to include the minimum-wage increase, which Mr. Biden has pledged to pass. An amendment offered by Sen. Bernie Sanders (I., Vt.) to the Senate bill to include the wage raise was defeated, with eight Democratic senators voting "no."\par \par \uc2 "We remain extremely disappointed that the minimum wage bill was not included," Ms. Jayapal said. "The minimum wage remains essential policy and we must deliver on this issue."\par \par \uc2 A provision on the minimum wage was left out of the Senate version after a February ruling by the Senate parliamentarian that it didn't comply with chamber rules for the aid measure.\par \par \uc2 The margin for the Democratic leadership is even tighter in the evenly divided Senate, which enabled Sen. Joe Manchin (D., W.Va.) to bring the debate on the bill to a halt Friday and eventually obtain changes in a $300 weekly unemployment benefit for workers laid off as a result of the pandemic. That benefit will now end in September, about a month earlier than provided under an agreement reached earlier among most Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Appearing on four political talk shows Sunday morning, Mr. Manchin praised the relief package and redoubled his opposition to calls from fellow Democrats who want to change Senate rules to allow more bills to pass with a simple majority vote, reducing Republicans' ability to block their proposals.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not going to change my mind on the filibuster," Mr. Manchin said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Manchin also rejected for now the suggestion of carve-outs to Senate rules to allow other Democratic legislation, such as the House-passed election-reform measure, to pass the Senate with a simple majority vote.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not going to go there until my Republican friends have the ability to have their say also," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Manchin said he would be open to changes that make procedural filibusters harder to employ. "If you want to make it a little bit more painful, make them stand there and talk, I'm willing to look at any way we can," he said on NBC.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Democrats have tried to emphasize the achievement of getting the Covid-19 relief bill through the 50-50 Senate, which Democrats captured in a pair of upset election wins in Georgia partly on the argument that the party could get serious legislation passed after years of gridlock.\par \par \uc2 "We passed the most significant bill in the history of the last 10 years, and every Democrat voted for it," Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer (D., N.Y.) said in an interview Saturday. "This was a very hard thing to get done."\par \par \uc2 But the debate laid bare the difficulty Mr. Schumer will face in getting his caucus to agree on policy.\par \par \uc2 Even among senators who agree that the minimum wage should be increased, disagreement remains. Mr. Manchin, for instance, has called for raising it to $11 an hour.\par \par \uc2 The wrangling over the Covid-19 relief bill could be an omen as Mr. Biden prepares to turn to other significant items on his first-year agenda. The administration has said it would start work on Mr. Biden's promise of a $2 trillion infrastructure package once the relief bill is signed.\par \par \uc2 And the changes made in the Senate to win support from lawmakers such as Mr. Manchin and \uc2 Sen. Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  (D., Ariz.) could imperil the relief package in the House if progressive Democrats decide to vote no.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Manchin demonstrated on Friday the leverage individual Democrats can wield by threatening to break from party leaders in such a narrowly divided chamber, even on those bills that can be passed with a simple majority.\par \par \uc2 He was pressed by fellow Democrats and received a phone call from Mr. Biden, but he insisted on cutting back the length of the extra unemployment benefits, saying the payments and added income-tax benefits needed to be better targeted to those who need them.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Manchin said he hadn't extracted any promises from Mr. Biden in exchange for his vote on the bill. "I've never done that," Mr. Manchin said. "I just don't trade off that way. There's no need to."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Manchin has Mr. Biden's support, for now. White House Communications Director \uc2 Kate Bedingfield\uc2  said on CNN that Mr. Biden doesn't support eliminating the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 "Look at what we have been able to do in the first six weeks that we have been in office with the filibuster in place," she said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210308eh3800020\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
