\par\par \b \uc2 Investing in Funds & ETFs: A Monthly Analysis --- The Wall Street Journal Tax Guide 2021 --- With the latest on pandemic changes, taxes on remote work and more\b0\par\par\uc2 B Laura Saunders, Richard Rubin and the staff of The Wall Street Journal \par \uc2 2,657 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Covid-19 pandemic made 2020 a tax year like no other. Now those issues are part of 2021, for both taxpayers filing 2020 returns and those planning ahead.\par \par \uc2 For filers with questions, The Wall Street Journal Tax Guide for 2021 provides information about the tax provisions affecting individuals for last year and this one. In addition to pandemic tax changes, it discusses other provisions and includes rates, brackets and other benchmarks.\par \par \uc2 At this moment, a key unresolved issue is whether the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  will postpone the April 15 tax due date as it did last year. Congress and professional groups such as the \uc2 American Institute of CPAs\uc2  have asked for such a delay, but so far \uc2 IRS\uc2  officials have said they don't plan to make a change. The only current exception is Texas and Oklahoma residents and business owners. They have until June 15 because of the storms that recently ravaged the states.\par \par \uc2 This pullout, foldable section includes highlights from this year's guide. For details on how to access the full guide online, see the last page.\par \par \uc2 Capital Gains:\par \par \uc2 Taxes for Investors\par \par \uc2 For investors using taxable accounts --as opposed to tax-sheltered retirement accounts such as IRAs or 401(k)s --the tax law has special rules that offer benefits and pitfalls.\par \par \uc2 When an investor sells a holding in a taxable account, the capital gain or loss is the difference between the original cost (plus adjustments) and the selling price. So if someone bought a share at $3 and sold it for $5, the capital gain is $2. If the investor bought another share for $3 and sold it for $2, the capital loss is $1.\par \par \uc2 A key benefit is that capital losses can offset taxable capital gains. So if the investor above sold both shares in the same year, she would have a net taxable gain of $1 instead of $2 for that year. And if total losses exceed total gains, the extra losses can offset up to $3,000 of income such as wages per year.\par \par \uc2 Unused capital losses can be carried forward into future years to offset gains.\par \par \uc2 Long-term capital gains are profits on investments held longer than a year, and they are taxed at favorable rates of 0, 15%, or 20%. Short-term gains are those on investments held a year or less, and they are taxed at the higher rates for ordinary income (like wages) that range from 10% to 37%. This is an important distinction for day traders to note.\par \par \uc2 The favorable lower rates for long-term gains also apply to dividends that are "qualified," which is most of them. They do not apply to interest income.\par \par \uc2 The 3.8% Surtax\par \par \uc2 A 3.8% surtax applies to net investment income for most single filers whose adjusted gross income (AGI) exceeds $200,000 and most couples filing jointly with AGI above $250,000.\par \par \uc2 For example, if a single filer has $150,000 of ordinary income plus a $60,000 taxable long-term gain plus $15,000 of qualified dividends, then $25,000 would be subject to the 3.8% surtax.\par \par \uc2 As a result, top-bracket taxpayers typically owe 23.8% instead of 20% on their long-term gains and dividends. And some investors in the 15% bracket for investment income owe the 3.8% surtax on part or all of it, if their adjusted gross income is above $200,000 for married joint filers or $200,000 for single filers.\par \par \uc2 For example, say that David is a single filer with $210,000 of adjusted gross income, and $50,000 of that is a windfall from a long-term gain on an investment and some qualified dividends.\par \par \uc2 In that case, David's investment income would likely be taxed at a 15% rate but he would owe an extra 3.8% on $10,000 because that is the amount of investment income above $200,000.\par \par \uc2 Thus his tax rate on the $10,000 would be 18.8%.\par \par \uc2 The Zero Tax Rate\par \par \uc2 Some people owe no taxes on their capital gains and dividends. Here is a simplified example: Say that Janet is a single taxpayer with $30,000 of taxable ordinary income for 2020 after deductions and exemptions, such as for tax-free municipal-bond interest or the sale of her home. Her taxable income is subject to rates of up to 12%, as detailed in the income-tax brackets.\par \par \uc2 But Janet also has a $20,000 long-term capital gain, and it "stacks" on top of her $30,000 of taxable income, giving her total taxable income of $50,000.\par \par \uc2 For 2020, the 15% bracket for capital gains begins at $40,000 of taxable income for single filers. As a result, Janet would owe zero tax on $10,000 of her gain and 15% on the remaining $10,000.\par \par \uc2 Cryptocurrency enforcement\par \par \uc2 Cryptocurrency owners, beware: The \uc2 IRS\uc2  is trying to strip away excuses for millions who might be ignoring tax rules.\par \par \uc2 On the 2020 tax return, a key question will appear on the front page of the 1040 in a prominent place, just below the taxpayer's name and address: At any time during 2020, did you sell, receive, send, exchange or otherwise acquire any financial interest in any virtual currency?\par \par \uc2 The taxpayer must check the box "Yes" or "No."\par \par \uc2 Cryptocurrency owners who don't answer the question or are untruthful risk higher penalties if they're audited, as it will be harder to claim they were unaware of the rules.\par \par \uc2 The new position of this question is the latest effort by the \uc2 IRS\uc2  to deter cryptocurrency tax cheating. In 2019 it sent letters to more than 10,000 crypto holders warning that they might not be in compliance and asking them to step forward, and is taking other steps as well.\par \par \uc2 The IRS first released guidance on cryptocurrencies in 2014, saying that bitcoin and its kin are property, not currencies. Often they are akin to stock shares or real estate, and sale proceeds are typically taxes as long- or short-term capital gains. Losses can be used to offset gains.\par \par \uc2 This means that using bitcoin to buy coffee -- or a car -- isn't like using cash. The transfer typically triggers a taxable gain or loss as a sale of stock would, and tax may be due.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, the \uc2 IRS\uc2  issued more guidance in this area, including controversial rules on splits known as forks.\par \par \uc2 In late 2020, the \uc2 Financial Crimes Enforcement Network\uc2  (\uc2 FinCEN\uc2 ), a Treasury Department unit separate from the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , announced that it may require U.S. taxpayers holding more than $10,000 of crypto assets offshore to file \uc2 FinCEN\uc2  Form 114, known as the FBAR, to report these holdings. This rule hasn't yet been adopted, so it wasn't in effect for 2020.\par \par \uc2 Pandemic Response:\par \par \uc2 What Was Changed\par \par \uc2 Congress and the \uc2 IRS\uc2  responded to the pandemic and lockdowns with a host of tax changes, although lawmakers didn't alter basic tax rates and brackets. Many taxpayers are coping with new or unfamiliar tax provisions this year, so here is a selection of changes to be aware of.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment compensation\par \par \uc2 Unemployment payments -- including extra amounts due to the pandemic -- are usually taxable, and recipients should receive a Form 1099-G that reports the 2020 total both to them and to the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . These forms were supposed to be mailed or made available by Jan. 31, 2021. However, Congress is poised to pass a tax exemption for the first $10,200 of 2020 unemployment payments per spouse for households making up to $150,000.\par \par \uc2 Some taxpayers have been victims of unemployment-payment ID theft and have received 1099-G forms for amounts they didn't file for or receive. The \uc2 IRS\uc2  has a webpage for these victims at IRS.gov.\par \par \uc2 Medical expenses\par \par \uc2 In late 2020, Congress also enacted a permanent threshold of 7.5% of adjusted gross income for taxpayers who want to deduct medical expenses on Schedule A. This means that filers who itemize can deduct eligible expenses only to the extent that they exceed 7.5% of their AGI. Without the change, the threshold would have risen to 10%.\par \par \uc2 Relatively few taxpayers benefit from this write-off because their expenses don't exceed the threshold. But it covers a range of unreimbursed costs when it does apply, and it is valuable to people with expenses for chronic or nursing-home care. Other eligible costs include insurance premiums paid with after-tax dollars, prostheses, eyeglasses and even a wig if needed after chemotherapy. For a long list of deductible costs, see \uc2 IRS\uc2  Publication 502.\par \par \uc2 State taxes on remote work\par \par \uc2 If you worked remotely in a state different from your usual one, you may have to file a tax return, and perhaps pay taxes, to more than one state. This is a complex area with rules that vary from state to state, and it will pose problems for many telecommuters whose offices closed in 2020. Some states also have special pandemic rules that will add more complications.\par \par \uc2 Think twice before cheating on this issue: Employers and tax preparers are unlikely to risk consequences by filing incorrect forms, and do-it-yourself filers should remember that tax returns are signed under penalty of perjury.\par \par \uc2 Flexible spending accounts\par \par \uc2 Flexible spending accounts (FSAs) are employer-sponsored plans that allow workers to set aside pretax dollars to pay for unreimbursed health expenses (such as glasses or over-the-counter medication) or certain dependent-care expenses, such as summer camp.\par \par \uc2 Many FSA participants didn't use all the money in their 2020 accounts because of the pandemic. At the end of 2020, however, Congress eased FSAs use-or-lose-it rules. Workers can now carry over unused funds from 2020 to 2021 or 2022. For dependent-care accounts, lawmakers extended the age limit from 12 to 13 for some carried-over funds.\par \par \uc2 For workers to take advantage of these changes, company plans often must opt into them.\par \par \uc2 Stimulus payments\par \par \uc2 Congress has made two rounds of stimulus payments totaling up to $1,800 per adult and $1,100 per dependent under age 17. These payments began to phase out at $75,000 of adjusted gross income for single filers and $150,000 for married joint filers. Lawmakers are poised to approve a third round of $1,400 payments for many people.\par \par \uc2 The first two rounds were upfront payments of a tax credit for 2020, but they were determined by income on taxpayers' 2018 or 2019 returns. As a result, some taxpayers can claim full or additional payments based on their 2020 tax return -- say, because their income dropped into the eligibility range or they had a child. This is known as the Recovery Rebate Credit, and it's on Line 30 of the 1040 and 1040-SR forms. There's a worksheet to determine amounts due the taxpayer.\par \par \uc2 Stimulus payments and Rebate Recovery Credits aren't taxable, and in most cases overpayments don't need to be returned to the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . However, the agency will be matching stimulus payments with rebate credits to detect double dippers.\par \par \uc2 The third round of stimulus payments will likely be based on 2019 or 2020 tax returns -- whatever is available when payments go out.\par \par \uc2 Charitable-donation deductions\par \par \uc2 For the 2020 tax year, Congress is allowing filers to deduct up to $300 in 2020 charitable donations if they don't break out their deductions on Schedule A. This deduction rises to $300 for single filers and $600 for married joint filers for 2021.\par \par \uc2 These donations must be in cash -- by check, credit card or similar transfer -- and they can't be property such as used clothing or furniture.\par \par \uc2 Also for 2020 and 2021, many taxpayers who make large cash donations can immediately deduct amounts up to 100% of their adjusted gross income. Absent this provision, the limit would be 60% of AGI. As in other years, excess donations carry forward for use in the future.\par \par \uc2 2020 RMDs from retirement plans\par \par \uc2 A year ago, Congress suspended the requirement that retirement-account holders take out at least some money once they hit a certain age. But by the time of the suspension, many savers had already made their annual RMD (required minimum distribution) from retirement accounts such as IRAs and 401(k)s. The \uc2 IRS\uc2  then said that such withdrawals could be returned to the accounts by Aug. 31, 2020.\par \par \uc2 Filers should treat these transactions as rollovers. That means reporting the required payouts on Line 4a for most IRAs and 5a for most other accounts. Then, if the saver put the entire payout back, record a zero on Line 4b or 5b and write the word "rollover" next to that or check the box on the software. If part of the payout wasn't put back, then put that amount on Line 4b or 5b instead of a zero.\par \par \uc2 Emergency retirement-plan withdrawals\par \par \uc2 In 2020, Congress allowed many people affected by the pandemic to make withdrawals of up to $100,000 from retirement plans such as 401(k)s, IRAs and Roth IRAs. Most important, lawmakers waived the 10% penalty that often applies to such withdrawals.\par \par \uc2 These payouts themselves are taxable, unless the saver puts the money back into the account or a similar one. The income from the withdrawals can be spread over three years, but there is room to strategize.\par \par \uc2 For example, say that someone affected by the pandemic withdrew $50,000 from an IRA last year and expects much lower income for 2020 than 2021 or 2022. If the saver thinks he or she won't put the money back, it might lower the overall taxes to declare the entire $50,000 as 2020 income, instead of spreading it over three years.\par \par \uc2 So far, Congress hasn't authorized such Covid-related withdrawals for 2021.\par \par \uc2 Checklist:\par \par \uc2 5 Ways You Can Still Save on 2020 Taxes\par \par \uc2 There are still a few things people can do now to cut last year's taxes. Here are some:\par \par \uc2 Contribute to a traditional IRA\par \par \uc2 Savers can take a tax deduction for putting up to $6,000 in a traditional IRA, and people age 50 and over can put in an extra $1,000. (Limits may apply based on type or amount of income.) The deadline for 2020 contributions is April 15, 2021, unless the \uc2 IRS\uc2  delays it further. Residents of Texas and Oklahoma have until June 15, 2021.\par \par \uc2 Contribute to a SEP IRA\par \par \uc2 People with self-employment income can often make a tax-deductible contribution for 2020 of up to $57,000 to these retirement accounts. The deadline for setting them up and contributing for 2020 is Oct. 15, 2021, for taxpayers who have a six-month extension to file.\par \par \uc2 Contribute to an HSA\par \par \uc2 Taxpayers covered by approved high-deductible healthcare plans for 2020 have until April 15, 2021, to make tax-deductible contributions to health savings accounts (HSAs) for 2020, or June 15 if they are in Texas or Oklahoma, unless the \uc2 IRS\uc2  delays the tax deadline further. The deduction for single coverage is up to $3,550, plus $1,000 for those age 55 and older. The deduction for family coverage is up to $7,100, plus $1,000 if the HSA owner is 55 or older.\par \par \uc2 Save on penalties\par \par \uc2 People who didn't pay in enough tax in 2020, and can't file a return now, can make a payment of part or all of the overdue tax to stop the penalty or lessen it. See IRS.gov/payments.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 More available\par \par \uc2 Business meals, alimony, "529" plans. . . There are tax issues with many facets of life. Subscribers can download the complete WSJ ebook on this year's key tax matters at WSJ.com/taxguide.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich243\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal3645
