\par\par \b \uc2 MTA\uc2  Considers Cuts in Subway Service --- Fare revenues are expected to stay below pre-pandemic levels for years\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Berger \par \uc2 848 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 March 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A9A\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Transit officials are debating whether to reduce service on New York City's subway and bus systems as ridership sputters during the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The state-controlled \uc2 Metropolitan Transportation Authority\uc2  has so far avoided massive cuts to subway and bus service thanks to $8 billion in federal Covid-19 relief and $2.9 billion of federal borrowing to cover operating costs.\par \par \uc2 Some officials say targeted reductions are needed with ridership and fare revenues expected to linger below pre-pandemic levels for years. Others argue that reductions must be weighed against the need to avoid crowding and lure back passengers.\par \par \uc2 Representatives for the \uc2 MTA\uc2  said the authority constantly monitors and re-evaluates ridership. "It's just a delicate public policy balance," said Abbey Collins, an MTA spokeswoman.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 MTA\uc2  reduced subway and bus service by 25% when the pandemic hit the city in the spring, as ridership fell 90% and the authority had a shortage of workers because of the virus. The authority has run almost full service since last summer, despite soaring deficits and continuing low ridership on some subway cars and buses.\par \par \uc2 Robert Foran\uc2 , the authority's chief financial officer, told \uc2 MTA\uc2  board members at a Feb. 18 meeting that "right-sizing" service would be needed if low ridership and budget deficits continue. "If the new normal is something less than pre-pandemic levels, it's a question of when we start to address it and adjust," he said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 MTA\uc2  has reduced commuter-rail service on the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  and Metro-North Railroad, saving tens of millions of dollars annually. But it is running almost full bus and subway service, despite respective ridership levels 50% and 30% of pre-pandemic norms.\par \par \uc2 Many riders on the subway and bus systems today are believed to be essential workers. Some white-collar employers, who have been slow to bring back workers, say they will continue to allow people to telecommute at least a few days a week after the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 MTA\uc2  believes that systemwide ridership on its buses, subway and commuter trains will return slowly, reaching about 60% of normal levels this time next year and about 86% of such levels by late 2024.\par \par \uc2 Some transit advocates and \uc2 MTA\uc2  board members say the authority shouldn't rush to reduce subway and bus service. They say the $8 billion of federal relief has bought the \uc2 MTA\uc2  time to consider where and when to reduce service.\par \par \uc2 Betsy Plum, executive director of advocacy group Riders Alliance, said the modest savings that come from service cuts are outweighed by the harm they do to riders.\par \par \uc2 Robert Linn, an \uc2 MTA\uc2  board member appointed by Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2 , said: "The \uc2 MTA\uc2  has a once-in-a-lifetime chance to re-envision transit in the metropolitan area and it needs time to start thinking about that."\par \par \uc2 Many transit agencies around the country are running reduced services to meet current low demand, according to the \uc2 American Public Transportation Association\uc2 , a trade group. \uc2 Paul Skoutelas\uc2 , the association's chief executive officer, said agencies are focused on how to reinstate buses and trains to meet commuting habits that have changed because of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Phil Washington, the chief executive of the Los Angeles County Metropolitan Transportation Authority, said his agency saves on maintenance and power costs as it runs 80% service to carry 55% of its pre-pandemic ridership. He said future schedules will likely reflect a new reality that more workers are commuting during off-peak hours and telecommuting at least a few days a week.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 MTA\uc2  is hoping for a further $8 billion of federal relief in the Biden administration's $1.9 trillion Covid-19 package, which was passed by the House on Saturday. As with previous requests, the authority warns of service cuts of 40% to 50%, beginning in 2023, if funding doesn't materialize.\par \par \uc2 Some \uc2 MTA\uc2  board members suggest the authority, which was in a fiscal crisis before the pandemic, could find workforce savings. Labor accounts for $10.7 billion, or 61%, of the \uc2 MTA\uc2 's budget.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 MTA\uc2  has trimmed more than 2,700 positions through attrition and a hiring freeze, reducing its workforce to less than 68,000 people. Yet the authority's $17.6 billion operating budget for 2021 is about $500 million more than last year's. Officials say rising costs are largely due to increases in health, welfare, pension, debt service and other expenses.\par \par \uc2 Transport Workers Union Local 100, which represents almost 40,000 New York City subway and bus workers, has challenges cost-cutting measures.\par \par \uc2 John Samuelsen\uc2 , TWU International President and an \uc2 MTA\uc2  board member, said he believes ridership will bounce back quicker than the authority forecasts. "They need to be thinking about every enticement possible to lure people out of their cars and onto the transit system," he said. "And you do that by maintaining and increasing service to a very high level of comfort as we pull out of Covid."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210302eh3200019\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
