\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Use of Tax to Raise Wages Is Ruled Out\b0\par\par\uc2 By Siobhan Hughes \par \uc2 800 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 March 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Senate Democrats will drop a plan to raise workers' wages through tax penalties and tax incentives that lawmakers had explored as a replacement for raising the minimum wage, according to three people familiar with the matter, while lawmakers race to pass a $1.9 trillion coronavirus relief package in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 Top Democrats had been looking at options, including imposing tax penalties on companies with at least $2.5 billion in gross receipts that aren't paying their workers at least $15 an hour, to raise workers' wages as part of the relief plan. An earlier effort to raise the minimum wage to $15 ran aground last week when the Senate's nonpartisan parliamentarian ruled that the wage hike did not comply with the guidelines that Democrats must follow to pass the legislation without Republican support. Top Senate Democrats had started working on the tax proposals to find a way to comply with Senate rules.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are aiming to complete the relief package before March 14, when federal unemployment benefits expire. The bill would fund vaccine distribution, enhance and extend federal unemployment benefits, and send direct checks of $1,400 to many Americans and $350 billion to state and local governments, among other measures.\par \par \uc2 Because of the tight timeline and the complexities of raising wages through the tax code, lawmakers will drop the plan to ensure quick passage of the overall bill, according to two of the people. Outside groups including unions and advocates had expressed concerns about the plan's workability and its policy implications, according to one of the people. Given the tight timeline before enhanced federal unemployment benefits run out in mid-March, lawmakers opted to set aside trying to fashion the new tax proposal.\par \par \uc2 The Washington Post earlier reported lawmakers' plans to drop the tax proposal.\par \par \uc2 Abandoning the wage effort will likely inflame tensions between the Democratic Party's progressive wing and its leadership. Raising the minimum wage to $15 an hour has been a priority for liberal Democrats, and the parliamentarian's ruling this week prompted some to call for the Senate to alter its rules and approve the wage increase anyway. President Biden has indicated he would not bend existing rules to raise the wage, and the end of the tax effort means Democrats will need to find an alternative legislative route for the measure.\par \par \uc2 The House approved its version of the legislation on Friday and included the $15 minimum wage in the bill. Lawmakers in the Senate will amend the bill and send it back to the House before it goes to Mr. Biden for his signature.\par \par \uc2 The plan to impose tax penalties on large companies that aren't paying a higher wage and create tax incentives for small companies to do so had faced skepticism from other Senate Democrats.\par \par \uc2 "I do want to make sure that a company would not simply move their work from the large corporate enterprise to a smaller enterprise that might not fall under that penalty and somehow have the work outsourced in a way that may not be appropriate," Sen. Mark Warner (D., Va.) said on "Fox News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 Other lawmakers had talked about reaching a bipartisan accord on the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 Sen. Chris Coons (D., Del.), who hasn't said whether he thinks $15 an hour is the right level for the minimum wage, said that he was willing to negotiate with other Democrats.\par \par \uc2 "Let's start with what we all agree on: that the federal minimum wage of $7.25 is too low," Mr. Coons said on CNN. "It has to be raised. Every Democrat and many Republicans agree with that."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Coons also said that Democrats might want to look at other avenues for raising the minimum wage outside the coronavirus relief package.\par \par \uc2 But Sen. Mazie Hirono (D., Hawaii), said, "I basically support raising the minimum wage in the Covid bill because that is a huge part of economic recovery. The people who are suffering most from the minimum wage and what's happening in the pandemic are the essential workers, many of them are women and many of them are getting paid very low wages."\par \par \uc2 Republicans say that the minimum-wage provision isn't relevant to dealing with a pandemic, and that in any event the Democrats' coronavirus-relief package is too expensive and is loaded down with money for unrelated projects, like $1.5 million for a bridge in New York. "There are things that have nothing to do with Covid," Sen. Rob Portman (R., Ohio) said on ABC's "This Week."\par \par \uc2 White House spokeswoman \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said the Biden administration had been receptive to GOP ideas, but none addressed the extent of the problem.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210301eh310001k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
