\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Democrats Seek Path on Wages --- Backup proposal would use tax code to prompt a pay boost at some big companies\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kristina Peterson, Richard Rubin and Eric Morath \par \uc2 778 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 February 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Senate Democrats began preparing a backup plan Friday for raising the minimum wage at some large companies as part of their coronavirus relief package, after advice from the chamber's parliamentarian ruled out including a broader increase.\par \par \uc2 As the House was set to pass a $1.9 trillion Covid-19 relief package, two committee chairmen backed by Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer (D., N.Y.) were working on a new, more limited proposal that would use the tax system to prod employers to raise wages.\par \par \uc2 The plan would impose tax penalties on large companies that aren't paying the higher wage Democrats seek and create tax incentives for small companies to do so. Democrats hope the chamber's parliamentarian would allow them to include the modified plan in the coronavirus relief package.\par \par \uc2 The tax plan is being developed by Senate Budget Committee Chairman Bernie Sanders (I., Vt.) and Senate Finance Committee Chairman \uc2 Ron Wyden\uc2  (D., Ore.), and they haven't finalized the details.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Wyden said Friday the proposal would impose a 5% penalty on the total payroll of a large corporation if it pays any workers below a certain amount, which he didn't specify, with the penalty increasing over time. The plan would also provide small businesses that pay their employees higher wages with an income-tax credit equal to 25% of wages, capped at $10,000 per employer a year.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Wyden didn't specify how large a company would have to be before it would face the tax, or how small it would have to be to get the credit.\par \par \uc2 "I've been working on a 'plan B' that would make big companies pay for mistreating their workers," Mr. Wyden said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sanders has said he is working on pushing companies to pay $15 an hour through a tax plan.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Schumer is looking at adding the tax measure to the coronavirus bill, according to a senior Democratic aide.\par \par \uc2 Some large employers, including \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Costco Wholesale Corp\uc2 ., have already raised pay to at least $15 an hour. Employers would have to decide whether they would fare better by raising wages to avoid the tax or by paying it. That decision would depend on the tax, the wage target and the cost of bringing the fraction of employees below that level up to it.\par \par \uc2 The Retail Industry Leaders Association\uc2 , which represents large companies, called Mr. Wyden's proposal a terrible idea. Republicans criticized the proposal as a tax increase that would harm businesses and reduce employment.\par \par \uc2 "It seems ludicrous to tax people for a job-killing measure," said \uc2 Rep. Kevin Brady\uc2  of Texas, the top Republican on the House Ways and Means Committee.\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said the Biden administration hadn't yet reviewed the tax proposal. "We are looking for the best ways to increase the minimum wage," she told reporters.\par \par \uc2 The impact of the tax proposal would depend on some of the still unspecified details, including whether the penalties apply to a handful of very large corporations or to a broader set of businesses.\par \par \uc2 To advance, the tax proposal would first have to secure the approval of all 50 members of the Senate Democratic caucus, who are using a process tied to the budget known as reconciliation to enable them to pass the $1.9 trillion aid bill with a simple majority. Using the process requires that measures be closely tied to the budget. The Senate parliamentarian said Thursday that Democrats' initial proposal to raise the federal minimum wage to $15 an hour over four years didn't comply with those rules.\par \par \uc2 It was unclear how centrist Democratic senators Joe Manchin of West Virginia and Kyrsten Sinema of Arizona, who opposed a broad $15 wage, would view the tax proposal. Mr. Manchin's office declined to comment and Ms. Sinema's office didn't respond to a request to comment.\par \par \uc2 Even if Senate Democrats were to unite around the proposal, progressive House Democrats said Friday that the tax proposal would be insufficient and that they would still press for a federal minimum wage increase.\par \par \uc2 "I'm very supportive of doing whatever we can, but at the end of the day we promised a $15 minimum wage, so if that $15 minimum wage isn't in this package, we are going to have to figure out a way to get it through," \uc2 Rep. Pramila Jayapal\uc2  (D., Wash.), chairwoman of the Congressional Progressive Caucus, told reporters Friday.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210227eh2r0002j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
