\par\par \b \uc2 C-Suite Strategies (A Special Report) --- Employers Want More Diversity. They May Need To Open Offices In New Places: Research on race in the workplace suggests executives could close racial gaps within their ranks by being more strategic about geographic locations\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chip Cutter \par \uc2 672 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 February 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Among the key takeaways from a new report on race in the workplace is this surprise: To address inequality inside organizations, some companies may need to open offices in new locations.\par \par \uc2 Blacks are a relatively small part of the population in some U.S. cities expected to see the highest job growth over the next decade, such as Seattle, and nearly 60% live in the South, according to a sweeping new analysis by researchers at the consulting firm \uc2 McKinsey & Co\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Workers need to be connected to the opportunities being created and have the skills for the in-demand roles," says \uc2 James Manyika\uc2 , a senior partner at McKinsey who is a director of the \uc2 McKinsey Global Institute\uc2  and helped lead the research. "But jobs are often concentrated in counties that aren't necessarily accessible to Black workers."\par \par \uc2 McKinsey studied detailed employment data for more than 3,000 U.S. counties and cross-referenced it with internal corporate data and interviews inside companies. What emerges is a comprehensive picture of Black representation in corporate America -- and the challenges that remain for both workers and organizations.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Manyika recently spoke with The Wall Street Journal. Here are edited excerpts of the conversation.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: How have the questions that executives ask you about diversity changed?\par \par \uc2 MR. MANYIKA: The general sense for most companies now is "We want to do this" as opposed to "Does this matter?" Where I'd like to see the questions move to is less "I'd like to hire the best African-American senior vice president, can you help me find one?" to "How do we train 100,000 African-Americans into managerial roles?"\par \par \uc2 WSJ: What stood out to you in this research?\par \par \uc2 MR. MANYIKA: A lot of Black employment in the private sector is concentrated in the South. Something like 60% of Black private-sector workers are in just 10 states. Population is a big part of that, but also history. Texas, Florida, Georgia and North Carolina make up about 30% of all Black employment. New York, California and Illinois add 17%.\par \par \uc2 In the counties that have higher employment rates in general -- that are growing jobs -- African-Americans are very underrepresented. They tend to be in the areas where there's either no job growth or declining occupations.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: Why is this important for companies?\par \par \uc2 MR. MANYIKA: If you want to solve this issue of race and employment in the private sector, it's actually an opportunity. In some of the low-growth counties, you have strong pipelines of talent, just not enough economic activity or investment by companies. So as companies think about "Where do we find talent?" they could be missing out.\par \par \uc2 Often, some of those counties are very close to large cities. So it could easily be putting up an office in those counties next to a very large city and having access to talent that's there.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: Companies have had diversity programs for a long time. Are they doing them wrong?\par \par \uc2 MR. MANYIKA: Certainly the companies in our survey, they're all trying to do the right thing. Part of the challenge may be not doing it enough at scale. If you look at the boards of directors and the C-suite, representation actually looks a little bit better than the middle of organizations.\par \par \uc2 Part of what I would encourage from this research is more targeting. Especially of that earlier set of transitions to managerial roles. I would also target how you create a sense of belonging.\par \par \uc2 People leave because they don't see themselves progressing to the next level or are leaving to try to start their own businesses.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cutter is a Wall Street Journal reporter based in New York. He can be reached at chip.cutter@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210222eh2m00004\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
