\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Democrats Face a Split on Minimum Wage\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Duehren \par \uc2 410 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 February 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Democrats are bracing for an internal battle over raising the minimum wage to $15 an hour, as they grappled with the political and procedural constraints on reaching a long-held goal of the party's progressive wing.\par \par \uc2 Raising the minimum wage to $15 an hour, which President Biden's $1.9 trillion coronavirus relief plan proposes to do by 2025, faces opposition from some Democratic lawmakers and parliamentary hurdles. Democrats cannot afford to lose a single vote in the 50-50 Senate on the relief package, which also calls for sending many Americans a $1,400 check, bolstering federal unemployment aid, and offering funds for vaccine distribution.\par \par \uc2 At a CNN town hall Tuesday, Mr. Biden floated the possibility of raising the minimum wage to $12 or $13 an hour by 2025, rather than $15. "I do support a $15 minimum wage," he said. "But that's a debatable issue."\par \par \uc2 While many Republicans have assailed the proposed increase, arguing it would hurt small businesses and cost jobs, Sens. \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  of Utah and Tom Cotton of Arkansas said they would soon introduce their own legislation to raise the minimum wage. The two Republicans said they would pair it with a requirement that employers verify immigration status of employees -- likely a nonstarter with Democrats.\par \par \uc2 In the Senate, two Democrats -- Sens. Joe Manchin of West Virginia, and Kyrsten Sinema of Arizona -- have said they don't support increasing the minimum wage to $15 an hour as part of the relief package. A larger group of 12 Democrats that includes Mr. Manchin and Ms. Sinema in the Senate hasn't signed onto a bill from Sen. Bernie Sanders (I., Vt.) raising the minimum wage to $15 an hour by 2025.\par \par \uc2 Among the ways Democrats could modify the proposal is changing the timeline for the wage increase or offering tax relief to small businesses to help offset new labor costs, according to lawmakers and aides.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats must first determine whether they will even be able to pass a minimum-wage increase under Senate rules. To skirt the 60-vote threshold for most legislation, Democrats are proceeding with reconciliation to approve the relief bill with a simple majority.\par \par \uc2 Senate rules prevent Congress from using reconciliation to approve measures that aren't directly related to the budget. The nonpartisan Senate parliamentarian rules on whether policies are eligible to be passed under reconciliation.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210218eh2i0001v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
