\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Wage Boost to Be In House Aid Bill\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kristina Peterson and Andrew Restuccia \par \uc2 534 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 February 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- House Democrats are preparing to stitch together a legislative version of President Biden's $1.9 trillion coronavirus relief proposal next week that will include an increase in the minimum wage to $15 an hour, despite a second Senate Democrat opposing it this week.\par \par \uc2 Committees spent the past week shaping portions of the legislation, including the proposal to gradually increase the federal minimum wage to $15 over four years. Early next week, the House Budget Committee is expected to assemble the pieces into one bill, which House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  (D., Calif.) said should pass the House by the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 Democrats can't afford to lose a vote from their ranks in the evenly divided Senate. But the office of \uc2 Sen. Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  (D., Ariz.) said this week that she wouldn't support including the wage increase in the relief package, joining Sen. Joe Manchin (D., W.Va.) and most Republicans in opposing its inclusion. Politico earlier reported her decision.\par \par \uc2 House Budget Committee Chairman \uc2 John Yarmuth\uc2  (D., Ky.) said in an interview Friday that the wage increase was always expected to be hard to pass. "We always knew it was going to be probably the roughest thing to both get through the floor" and procedural constraints, he said.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are trying to use a process known as reconciliation that would allow the Senate to pass the relief bill with a simple majority, rather than the usual 60 votes. It is unclear whether the wage increase is eligible to be passed this way.\par \par \uc2 If the wage increase is stripped out in the Senate, discussions are already under way about tackling it separately, Mr. Yarmuth said: "I would almost guarantee there will be a standalone bill if this doesn't make it through." A standalone bill would likely require 60 votes to clear procedural hurdles in the Senate, where Republicans have largely opposed it.\par \par \uc2 Republicans say raising the minimum wage will harm small businesses during the pandemic and destroy jobs. A report from the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  this week said that raising the minimum wage to $15 by 2025 could deliver raises for 27 million workers and lift 900,000 Americans above the poverty threshold, but it could cost 1.4 million Americans their jobs.\par \par \uc2 "There's a lot of ramifications from this $15 Washington mandate," said Rep. Jason Smith of Missouri, the top Republican on the Budget Committee, who said it could also raise expenses for seniors living on a fixed income.\par \par \uc2 Some supporters of a higher wage have signaled they would like to postpone the first increase until after the pandemic, while others want to lengthen the phase-in until 2030.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats' proposal, which Mr. Biden supports, calls for the minimum wage to increase to $9.50 an hour the day the law goes into effect, followed by annual increases over four years until the wage reaches $15 an hour. After that, the minimum wage would be adjusted annually to keep pace with median wage growth. The last increase in the federal minimum wage came in 2009.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Richard Rubincontributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210213eh2d0001w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
