\par\par \b \uc2 Office Reopenings Pushed Back to Latter Part of 2021 --- Returns are pushed to September or beyond, keeping firms and employees in limbo\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chip Cutter \par \uc2 788 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 February 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 One of the hardest questions for American corporations to answer: When should offices reopen?\par \par \uc2 From Silicon Valley to Tennessee to Pennsylvania, high hopes that a rapid vaccine rollout in early 2021 would send millions of workers back into offices by spring have been scuttled. Many companies are pushing workplace return dates to September -- and beyond -- or refusing to commit to specific dates, telling employees it will be a wait-and-see remote-work year.\par \par \uc2 The delays span industries. \uc2 Qurate Retail Inc\uc2 ., the parent company of brands such as \uc2 Ballard Designs\uc2 , \uc2 QVC\uc2  and \uc2 HSN\uc2 , recently shifted its planned May return to offices in the Philadelphia area, Atlanta and other cities until September at the earliest. TechnologyAdvice, a marketing firm in Nashville, initially told employees to plan on Feb. 1 as their return date. The company then pushed the date to August. Now, TA has decided it will begin a hybrid in-office schedule in the fall of 2021, letting workers choose whether to work remotely or come in, the company says.\par \par \uc2 Return-to-office dates have shifted so much in the past year that some companies aren't sharing them with employees. Shipping giant \uc2 United Parcel Service Inc\uc2 ., based in Atlanta, and financial-services firm \uc2 Fidelity Investments Inc\uc2 ., based in Boston, haven't announced return dates, instead telling workers signing on from home that the companies are monitoring the coronavirus pandemic and will call workers back when it is safe.\par \par \uc2 Nearly a year of makeshift work at home has weighed on employees, leaders say. While many companies say productivity is up, executives worry that creativity is suffering and say that burnout is on the rise. Even so, bosses struggle to say when things will change.\par \par \uc2 "Everyone's in the moment of limbo. They want certainty, but they know they can't have it," says Elizabeth Mygatt, an associate partner at consulting firm \uc2 McKinsey & Co\uc2 ., who advises companies on organizational performance. Companies that assigned return dates last year now hesitate to do so, she adds. "I have seen fewer companies be actually super clear on what the future looks like," she says.\par \par \uc2 A new survey of 2,200 U.S. workers by the \uc2 Conference Board\uc2 , a research group, found that 44% of employees polled didn't know their company's plans to return to the workplace. That is up from last September, when 37% of respondents polled by the group said they were unclear on their back-to-the-office plan.\par \par \uc2 Many executives say it has become tougher to predict when white-collar employees should go back to offices than it was last year. They cite news about a slower-than-hoped vaccine rollout, the fast spread of mutant virus strains and polls that show many workers remain anxious about in-person settings.\par \par \uc2 "Sometime in 2022 is where I'm thinking" all staffers will feel comfortable coming back, says Khalid Parekh, chief executive of Amsys Group, a Houston technology company that employs about 350 people. "Then we can feel safe."\par \par \uc2 Labor Day is an increasingly popular target date for employers. \uc2 Alphabet Inc\uc2 .'s \uc2 Google\uc2  recently delayed its expected return date to September, from July. \uc2 Grant Thornton LLP\uc2 , one of the biggest accounting and consulting firms in the U.S., expects most of its 8,500 workers to return to the office with flexible schedules in late summer or early fall.\par \par \uc2 ZocDoc Inc\uc2 ., an online doctor-appointments service that employs 550 people, plans to reopen its New York City office after Labor Day. By then, CEO \uc2 Oliver Kharraz\uc2  says he expects many of his employees will be vaccinated. "In some ways, Labor Day is a completely arbitrary date," he says. "On the other hand, it's the start of a new school year."\par \par \uc2 Continued school closures across much of the U.S. have created great difficulties for working parents, and some executives say they think a complete return to the office will be tough until schools are fully back in session.\par \par \uc2 Current office-occupancy rates are highest in parts of the country where large school districts have reopened, according to data from \uc2 Kastle Systems\uc2 , a security firm that monitors access-card swipes in more than 2,500 office buildings, from skyscrapers to suburban office campuses.\par \par \uc2 Right now, that means Texas: In Dallas, Austin and Houston, major school districts have offered in-person learning for many months, and offices are roughly 35% full, according to Kastle. By comparison, in New York City, where schools are open part-time for in-person learning, office occupancy is less than 15%.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Emily Glazer and Paul Ziobro contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210212eh2c0004u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
