\par\par \uc2 Heard on the Street\par \b \uc2 Uber, \uc2 Lyft\uc2  Surge Pricing Looks Excessive --- Recent share price returns suggest the ride-sharing companies are revved up for a recovery that may well run out of gas\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Forman \par \uc2 667 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 February 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 Consumers avoided ride-sharing last year like, well, the plague. Investors act as if there won't be any lasting damage.\par \par \uc2 Last spring, business evaporated overnight and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  said ride-share rides were down by more than 50% in January from a year earlier on its fourth-quarter conference call Tuesday. \uc2 Uber Technologies\uc2  said Wednesday on its quarterly call that mobility gross bookings were 43% lower in January. Investors still seem revved up for a ride-share recovery.\par \par \uc2 Uber shares rallied more than 95% over the past six months, benefiting from the increase in its food-delivery business amid the pandemic, but even \uc2 Lyft\uc2 's shares are up 82% over that period, significantly outperforming the market despite the lack of a similar unit. Those gains for a ride-share pure play in the face of a business that is severely depressed don't merely imply that ride-share will return to normal as the pandemic eases, but that it will emerge better than ever.\par \par \uc2 Investors should consider some defensive driving. The big shift from car ownership to transportation-as-a-service will likely resume after the pandemic eases, but perhaps more slowly than investors believe. For one thing, people bought a fair number of new cars last year thanks to low interest rates and the need for an escape route from cities. Meanwhile used-vehicle sales in the U.S. rose 17% above pre-pandemic forecasts in June, according to J.D. Power.\par \par \uc2 Fresh data from real-estate giant Zillow published earlier this month shows people left some of the biggest metro areas like San Francisco and New York in droves for suburbs and Midwestern cities. Many will return, but not all. Permanent work-from-home policies put in place by companies such as \uc2 Salesforce\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and \uc2 Zillow Group\uc2  mean some employees will have less of a need to use ride-sharing for daily commuting. They won't be traveling as much for business either, leading to a permanent decline for yet another major use case for ride-sharing.\par \par \uc2 On \uc2 Lyft\uc2 's conference call, Chief Executive \uc2 Logan Green\uc2  acknowledged that some pandemic changes are here to stay, including the work-from-home trend. But he called the notion that cities are dead "hogwash," noting that the exit of some people will make room for new, younger residents to come in. That may not necessarily be the case, though, with massive companies like \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  now saying they may pare down office space, including in cities like their home base of San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 The pace of recovery is likely to vary by region. Uber said on Wednesday that its mobility trends in Brazil are nearly back to pre-pandemic levels, for example. In the U.S., \uc2 Lyft\uc2  said on Tuesday that rides in West Coast cities have remained weak, while Texas and Florida rebounded.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2  said it expected to begin to see improving comparisons beginning in the second quarter andalso noted that aggressive fixed cost-cutting could lead to profits on an adjusted earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortization basis as early as the third quarter of this year. Uber still says it is expecting profitability on the same basis by the end of the year and noted that it should see strong growth and expanding margins in the second half of the year, assuming it hits management goals and the pandemic recedes.\par \par \uc2 Those who believe Covid-19 will be largely vanquished should consider the differences in \uc2 Lyft\uc2  and Uber's business models.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2 's business stands to see unmitigated upside in that scenario, while the very factors driving a ride-share recovery could dent Uber's food-delivery business, which has been so critical to its improving bottom line.\par \par \uc2 Investors in both businesses are clearly taking a ride or die approach.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich371\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal5565
